var e=[{id:`mj-QvSDRRGv8cs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:0,title:`.NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:52`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The Upgrade That Gaslit the Compiler: Aspire 8.2 → 9.2`,paragraphs:[`It's the night before the release branch freezes, and Milan's five-project Aspire solution — a Postgres database, a RabbitMQ broker, two APIs, and a frontend — builds green on 8.2. He retargets every csproj to net9.0, slams 'Update All' in the NuGet manager, and the build dies with an error he's never seen: the app host requires an Aspire AppHost SDK reference, version 9 or greater. The error message, bless it, hands him the exact line of XML to paste. He pastes it. That's when the compiler starts gaslighting him.`,`WaitFor is suddenly ambiguous — the community package he added back on 8.x to wait for healthy resources now collides with the WaitFor that Aspire 9 ships in the box. He deletes the package and the ambiguity clears. Then the project references — which are right there, correct, visible in Solution Explorer — stop resolving. The folk remedy on every thread: close Visual Studio and reopen it. He complies like a pagan at an altar. It works. Then he removes the now-optional IsAspireHost property and the ghosts come back — because the real exorcism isn't the restart, it's targeting the 9.2.0 AppHost SDK.`,`By the time the build is green, he's earned the toy: the new Resource Graph in the 9.2 dashboard. He adds Aspire.Hosting.Redis, declares a content-platform-cache resource, and watches the new node light up with its edges to the APIs. The upgrade that could have been a two-day archaeology dig took one focused hour — because every error, read carefully, contained its own fix.`],problem:`Now if I were to build my project at this point I would run into a problem: an ambiguous WaitFor call — the legacy wait-for-healthy NuGet package colliding with Aspire 9's built-in support — followed by project references that refuse to resolve once IsAspireHost is removed without bumping the AppHost SDK to 9.2.0.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire 9.2`,`AppHost SDK`,`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`Redis`,`Resource Graph`,`Visual Studio`],note:`The AppHost is the single composition root that declares every resource — Postgres, RabbitMQ, and the newly added Redis cache — and wires service discovery to two APIs and a frontend. Aspire 9.2's dashboard Resource Graph turns that declaration into a live topology map.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does one SDK line gate the whole build?`,body:[`Here's the thing that surprised me: the AppHost isn't just a project with some packages — it's built by a dedicated project SDK. When you move to Aspire 9, that SDK must be declared explicitly with an Aspire.AppHost.Sdk reference, version 9 or greater, right after the Project tag. The error message literally hands you the XML. My rule: when the compiler writes your fix for you, paste it — don't retype it, don't Google it first.`,`The second trap is the IsAspireHost property. From 9.2.0 it's no longer required, so of course I want it gone — dead properties are upgrade debt. But 'no longer required by the SDK' is not the same as 'your tooling has forgotten it exists.' Visual Studio's project system caches assumptions, and the moment I strip the property on an older SDK, project references that are demonstrably correct stop resolving. Two exits: restart VS (folk remedy, non-deterministic) or bump the AppHost SDK to 9.2.0 (deterministic). I want the deterministic one.`],quote:`An error message that contains its own fix is a gift — paste it before you Google it.`,table:{headers:[`Fix`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Keep IsAspireHost`,`Zero friction, VS stays happy`,`Dead property, upgrade debt`,`Quick patch`],[`Remove + restart VS`,`Clean csproj`,`Folk remedy, non-deterministic`,`Last resort`],[`Remove + SDK 9.2.0`,`Clean csproj, deterministic`,`Must bump SDK version`,`Right call`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — .NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvSDRRGv8cs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the ambiguous WaitFor`,body:[`Back on Aspire 8, waiting for a resource to become healthy wasn't built in — you pulled in a NuGet package that added WaitFor-style support. On 9.x, WaitFor ships with the platform. So after 'Update All,' I've got two extension methods with overlapping signatures in scope, and the compiler throws up its hands: ambiguous call. The temptation is to disambiguate — fully qualify, add a using alias. Resist it. The right move is deletion.`,`Why delete instead of keep? Because the community package is now a shadow of a platform feature: it won't get the dashboard health integration, it will drift, and every future contributor has to know which WaitFor is which. When the platform ships the thing you vendored, your vendored copy stops being a dependency and starts being a bug.`],quote:`When the platform ships the feature you vendored, your vendored copy becomes a compile error.`,table:{headers:[`Wait strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Community wait package`,`Worked on 8.x`,`Ambiguous on 9.x, drifts from platform`,`Delete it`],[`Built-in WaitFor`,`First-class, dashboard-integrated`,`Requires Aspire 9.x`,`Default`],[`No wait at all`,`Fastest startup`,`Race conditions on cold dependencies`,`Never in dev`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — .NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvSDRRGv8cs`,at:`0:31`}},{heading:`03 · The Resource Graph: from list to topology`,body:[`The headline feature of 9.2 is the Resource Graph in the dashboard, and I'll admit I underestimated it. My AppHost declares six resources — Postgres, RabbitMQ, the new Redis cache, two APIs, and a frontend. In the old list view that's six rows of green checkmarks; the structure is invisible. The graph shows the edges: which API reads Postgres, which one publishes to RabbitMQ, where the cache sits. That's the difference between a roster and a map.`,`Why does this matter operationally? Because incident triage starts with topology, not logs. 'The reporting API can't reach the database' has two very different root causes: the node is unhealthy, or the edge was never declared. The graph answers that in one glance — and it's the first thing I check after adding a resource, to confirm the wires I think I declared actually exist.`],quote:`A dependency you can see is a dependency you can debug.`,table:{headers:[`Dashboard view`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`List view`,`Dense, sortable, familiar`,`Hides relationships between resources`,`Health scan`],[`Resource graph`,`Shows edges, topology at a glance`,`Spatial cost as resource count grows`,`Triage & onboarding`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — .NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvSDRRGv8cs`,at:`1:07`}},{heading:`04 · The upgrade order that actually works`,body:[`Let me be honest about the order, because I got it wrong once: retarget every csproj to net9.0 first, then update packages, then add the AppHost SDK reference, then remove IsAspireHost, then delete the community wait package, then bump the AppHost SDK to 9.2.0. Packages before TFM and you get framework mismatch noise. IsAspireHost removed before the SDK bump and you get phantom reference errors. Each skipped step doesn't add an error — it multiplies them.`,`With the build green, the fun part: add Aspire.Hosting.Redis, call AddRedis for the content-platform-cache, and watch the graph grow a node. Small, verifiable steps — each one buildable, each one revertible — beat one heroic big-bang commit every single time. That's not caution; it's bisectability.`],quote:`Migrations fail in the steps you skip, not the steps you take.`,table:{headers:[`Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Big-bang upgrade`,`One commit, one review`,`Compounding errors, hard to bisect`,`Tiny apps`],[`Staged upgrade`,`Each step verifiable and revertible`,`More commits, more discipline`,`Anything shared`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — .NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvSDRRGv8cs`,at:`4:06`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Resource topology the 9.2 dashboard now draws`,caption:`One AppHost declaring six resources — the graph shows the edges the APIs actually use, not just a list of green checkmarks.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    AH["Aspire AppHost<br/>SDK 9.2.0"] --> PG[("Postgres")]
    AH --> MQ{{"RabbitMQ"}}
    AH --> RD[("Redis<br/>content-platform-cache")]
    AH --> API["Content Platform API"]
    AH --> RPT["Reporting API"]
    AH --> UI["Content Platform Presentation"]
    API -- "service discovery" --> PG
    API -- "pub/sub" --> MQ
    API -- "cache" --> RD
    RPT -- "service discovery" --> PG
    RPT -- "consumes events" --> MQ
    UI -- "HTTP" --> API`},{id:`upgrade-flow`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The upgrade decision flow, 8.2 → 9.2`,caption:`Every dead end in the migration has a one-line exit — the flow only hurts when you take the branches out of order.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Start: Aspire 8.2 on net8.0"] --> B["Retarget all csproj to net9.0"]
    B --> C["Update All NuGet packages"]
    C --> D{"AppHost SDK error?"}
    D -- "yes" --> E["Add Aspire.AppHost.Sdk 9.x<br/>after Project tag"]
    D -- "no" --> F["Remove IsAspireHost property"]
    E --> F
    F --> G{"Build green?"}
    G -- "no: ambiguous WaitFor" --> H["Delete community wait package"]
    H --> I{"References resolve?"}
    I -- "no" --> J["Target AppHost SDK 9.2.0<br/>restart Visual Studio"]
    I -- "yes" --> K["Green build"]
    J --> K
    G -- "yes" --> K
    K --> L["Add Aspire.Hosting.Redis + cache resource"]
    L --> M["Open dashboard Resource Graph"]`}],takeaways:[`Sequence the migration: TFMs → packages → SDK reference → drop IsAspireHost → delete wait package → SDK 9.2.0; each skipped step multiplies errors and roughly triples debug time.`,`One line unblocks the build: <Sdk Name="Aspire.AppHost.Sdk" Version="9.0" /> immediately after the <Project> tag — paste it from the error message, never retype it.`,`Delete any community wait-for-healthy package the moment you hit Aspire 9 — the built-in WaitFor turns it into an ambiguous-call compile error, not a deprecation warning.`,`IsAspireHost is only safely removable on AppHost SDK ≥ 9.2.0; phantom unresolved project references mean bump the SDK (or restart VS) before touching a single line of code.`,`Triage with the Resource Graph before logs: in a 6-resource app (2 APIs, 3 backing services, 1 frontend), a missing edge explains most 'dependency unreachable' incidents in one glance.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — .NET Aspire 9.2 is out and it's amazing! NEW RESOURCE GRAPH`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QvSDRRGv8cs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-c_YZazjQrNs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:1,title:`Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:26`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`EF Core`,`Dapper`,`pgvector`,`.NET`,`C#`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`2 AM: Our Search Started Recommending Articles We Deleted Three Days Ago`,paragraphs:[`It's 2:14 AM and the pager lights up: customers are seeing articles they deleted three days ago in the 'related reading' rail. Worse, the RAG chatbot is confidently quoting a pricing page we retired last quarter. The embeddings are stale, and nobody can say by how much.`,`Here's the setup that got us there. Postgres held the source of truth, a managed vector database held the embeddings, and a sync worker bridged the two through an outbox table. One failed batch, one silent retry loop, and the two stores drifted apart. Every search result was now a join across two databases that disagreed about reality.`,`The fix wasn't a better sync worker — it was deleting the sync worker. We enabled the pgvector extension on Postgres 17, stored a vector(1024) column right next to the articles it described, and embedded queries with the Qwen3-embedding 0.6B model running on Ollama. One database, one transaction, one truth. The entire drift bug class ceased to exist.`],problem:`Semantic search needs embeddings stored beside the rows they describe, but a dual-write sync between Postgres and a dedicated vector DB kept drifting. We needed similarity search, recommendations, and RAG grounding without a second database to keep honest.`},tech:{stack:[`Postgres 17`,`pgvector`,`pgvector.NET`,`Npgsql`,`Dapper`,`EF Core`,`.NET Aspire`,`Ollama`,`Qwen3 0.6B`,`Microsoft.Extensions.AI`,`RAG`],note:`pgvector adds a vector data type to Postgres so embeddings are stored, indexed, and queried next to the rows they describe; pgvector.NET maps that type into .NET over Npgsql (with Dapper and EF Core adapters), while Aspire wires the Postgres and Ollama containers and Qwen3-embedding 0.6B supplies the 1,024-dimension vectors.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just buy a vector database?`,body:[`Let me guess your first instinct, because it was mine: 'we need vectors, so we need a vector database.' That's how you end up with a managed cluster, a second bill, and — the part nobody warns you about — a sync job whose entire purpose is keeping two databases in agreement about reality. And sync jobs fail quietly.`,`pgvector flips the question. It's an extension that gives Postgres a new vector data type — a high-dimensional array — so you can store, index, and query embeddings right next to the rows they describe. No second store, no outbox, no drift. The article and its embedding commit in the same transaction or not at all.`,`To be fair to the dedicated databases: they earn their keep at serious scale — billions of vectors, exotic memory tuning, vector-only workloads. But for recommendations, similarity search, and RAG grounding over data that already lives in Postgres? You probably already own the database you need.`],quote:`The most expensive part of a second database isn't the query engine — it's keeping it honest.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Dedicated vector DB`,`Purpose-built ANN, independent scaling`,`Sync drift, second bill, no SQL joins`,`Billions of vectors, vector-only workloads`],[`pgvector in Postgres`,`ACID, joins with relational data, one deploy`,`ANN tuning is on you, shares DB resources`,`Millions of vectors, search + RAG`],[`App-side brute force`,`Zero infra, trivial to build`,`O(n) scan per query, no index`,`Prototypes, tiny datasets`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: teaching .NET what a vector is`,body:[`So how do vectors cross the boundary into C#? There's a pgvector.NET library under the pgvector organization, built on Npgsql — the native Postgres client for .NET. On top of that baseline it ships adapters for Dapper, EF Core, and even F#. I'm using Dapper today because I want to see the SQL, including the distance operator, but EF Core is the most popular route.`,`Two registrations make or break this. First, on the NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder you call UseVector() — that enables the data mapping for the vector type when writing to Postgres. Second, because I'm on Dapper, SqlMapper.AddTypeHandler with a new VectorTypeHandler, so Dapper knows how to translate a C# value into that column. Skip either one and you find out at runtime, mid-request — not at compile time.`,`That's the boundary tax, and it's small: two lines. Compare that to teaching your team a second database's client SDK, its auth model, and its sync semantics.`],quote:`Your ORM doesn't know a vector until you teach it — and it fails at runtime, not compile time.`,table:{headers:[`Mapping option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Dapper + type handler`,`Explicit SQL, you see the distance operator`,`Manual handler registration`,`Query-heavy, hand-tuned SQL`],[`EF Core`,`LINQ, migrations, most popular path`,`Less control over raw ANN queries`,`CRUD-first applications`],[`Raw Npgsql`,`Baseline, zero abstraction`,`Most boilerplate`,`Libraries and micro-benchmarks`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,at:`1:46`}},{heading:`03 · Picking the model: dimensions are a contract`,body:[`Before any of this works, something has to turn text into vectors. I'm running the Qwen3 embedding family locally through Ollama — specifically the 0.6B model, which outputs 1,024-dimension embeddings. The family comes in different parameter sizes, and they differ in output dimensions too, and that number matters more than people expect.`,`Here's why: the dimension count gets baked into your column — vector(1024) — and into your index. Pick a different model next quarter and you're re-embedding every row and rewriting the schema. Dimensions are a contract with your future self.`,`Running it through Ollama locally also means no per-token bill while you develop, and in Aspire I can give it a persistent lifetime and a data volume so the model weights survive restarts. Got a GPU? Enable GPU support — embedding latency drops dramatically.`],quote:`Pick dimensions like you pick a primary key: changing your mind later costs a rewrite of every row.`,table:{headers:[`Choice`,`Upside`,`Cost`,`Rule of thumb`],rows:[[`Smaller model (0.6B)`,`Fast, runs locally, cheap`,`Slightly lower recall quality`,`Default for most apps`],[`Larger embedding models`,`Better semantic nuance`,`Slower, bigger vectors, pricier index`,`Hard, nuanced domains`],[`Fewer dimensions`,`Smaller index, faster scans`,`Less expressiveness`,`Storage-bound systems`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,at:`3:29`}},{heading:`04 · Orchestrating it with Aspire: the whole stack in one AppHost`,body:[`Now the wiring, and this is where Aspire shines. In the AppHost I declare an Ollama resource with a persistent lifetime and a data volume, add the qwen3-embedding 0.6b model to it, and declare a Postgres resource — and here's the detail that saves you an afternoon: use the pgvector/pgvector image on Postgres 17, because vanilla Postgres does not ship the extension.`,`The Postgres resource exposes port 6432 locally, gets a data volume and a consistent lifetime for faster restarts, and hosts a database called articles. The API project references both resources with WithReference and waits for them to be available before starting. On the client side: AddOllamaApiClient for the model, AddNpgsqlDataSource for Postgres with UseVector in the configure delegate, and an IEmbeddingGenerator registered from Microsoft.Extensions.AI so embedding calls are one interface away.`,`The payoff: a fresh clone of the repo goes from zero to a running vector search stack with one command. No README with twelve manual steps — that's twelve ways it breaks on Monday.`],quote:`If your stack needs twelve manual steps to boot, that's twelve ways it breaks on Monday.`,table:{headers:[`Wiring approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Aspire AppHost`,`One-command boot, resource refs + wait-for-ready, dashboard`,`Newer tooling to learn`,`.NET dev stacks`],[`docker-compose`,`Ubiquitous, language-agnostic`,`Manual wiring, no wait-for-ready`,`Mixed non-.NET teams`],[`Manual local install`,`Zero abstraction`,`Drifts per machine, undocumented`,`Throwaway prototypes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,at:`5:57`}},{heading:`05 · The payoff: one query, one database`,body:[`And now the part we came for. A search request hits the API, we embed the query with the same IEmbeddingGenerator — same model, same 1,024 dimensions, that's non-negotiable — and then it's one SQL statement: order articles by the cosine distance between their embedding and the query vector, limit ten. pgvector's <=> operator does the math; the HNSW index keeps it from scanning everything.`,`But here's the part I actually love, and it's the thing a standalone vector store can't give you: the embeddings live in the same table as the rows. So you can filter with ordinary SQL before the nearest-neighbor scan — published = true, tenant_id = @tenant, category IN (...). Vector search becomes powerful the moment it can use a WHERE clause.`,`From there, the same primitive powers all three use cases we started with: similarity search over articles, recommendations via nearest neighbors, and RAG — retrieve the top-k chunks and hand them to the model as grounded context. One database, one query pattern, three features.`],quote:`Vector search gets powerful the moment it can use a WHERE clause.`,table:{headers:[`Use case`,`How pgvector serves it`,`Key piece`],rows:[[`Similarity search`,`ORDER BY embedding <=> query LIMIT k`,`HNSW index`],[`Recommendations`,`Embed item or user, find nearest neighbors`,`vector(1024) column`],[`RAG grounding`,`Retrieve top-k chunks as LLM context`,`IEmbeddingGenerator`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,at:`6:50`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One AppHost, two resources, one source of truth`,caption:`Aspire boots the API, a pgvector-enabled Postgres, and a local Ollama embedding model — embeddings live in the same database as the rows they describe.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph HOST["Aspire AppHost"]
    API["Articles API - .NET"]
    PG[("Postgres 17 + pgvector<br/>db: articles - port 6432")]
    OLL[("Ollama<br/>qwen3-embedding 0.6B")]
  end
  U["Client"] --> API
  API -- "Npgsql + UseVector()" --> PG
  API -- "IEmbeddingGenerator" --> OLL
  PG --- IDX["articles.embedding vector(1024) + HNSW index"]
  OLL --- DIM["1,024-dim embeddings"]`},{id:`query-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a similarity search request`,caption:`Embed the query with the same model, then let Postgres rank rows by cosine distance — filtered by ordinary SQL before the ANN scan.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant A as Articles API (.NET)
  participant O as Ollama - Qwen3 0.6B
  participant P as Postgres 17 + pgvector
  C->>A: GET /search?q=pooling Npgsql connections
  A->>O: Embed query via IEmbeddingGenerator
  O-->>A: float[1024]
  A->>P: SELECT id, title, dist FROM articles WHERE published ORDER BY dist LIMIT 10
  Note over P: Cosine distance via pgvector operator - ANN scan over HNSW index
  P-->>A: Top-10 rows with distances
  A-->>C: Ranked results or RAG context`}],takeaways:[`Reach for pgvector before a dedicated vector DB: one Postgres instance with an HNSW index serves semantic search well into millions of vectors, and you keep ACID transactions plus SQL joins over the same rows.`,`Treat embedding dimensions as a schema contract — qwen3-embedding:0.6B emits 1,024 dims, so declare vector(1024); switching models later means re-embedding and rewriting every row.`,`Register both mapping hooks before your first query: UseVector() on the NpgsqlDataSourceBuilder and SqlMapper.AddTypeHandler(new VectorTypeHandler()) for Dapper — miss either and it fails at runtime, not compile time.`,`Run Ollama with a persistent lifetime and a data volume in Aspire so model weights survive restarts, and flip on GPU support when available to cut embedding latency dramatically.`,`Start from the pgvector/pgvector Postgres 17 image, not vanilla Postgres — the extension ships pre-installed, and hand-installing extensions on a live cluster is a 3 AM migration waiting to happen.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Build Vector Search in .NET with Postgres and pgvector`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=c_YZazjQrNs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`ai-rag`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-F7nnCsIsVi0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:2,title:`Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:14`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`],story:{headline:`The purchase order that vanished between three services at 3 AM`,paragraphs:[`3:12 AM. A customer's purchase order was accepted by the Stocks API — and then it simply never showed up in Risk Management. Three services, three log streams, and a Slack thread asking 'did anyone see order 4f2a?'`,`I grepped all three files. The Stocks API had the ID buried inside a sentence, the consumer logged it in a different format, Reporting had nothing at all. Forty minutes of eyeballing text later, the culprit turned out to be the third-party pricing API returning a price above the limit — a decision the consumer made silently, in a log line nobody could query.`,`The fix wasn't more logs. It was making every log argument a queryable property, shipping them all to one place, and letting a trace ID stitch the HTTP hop to the RabbitMQ hop. That's the difference between reading logs and interrogating them.`],problem:`One order, three services, zero correlation: interpolated strings make the order ID unqueryable, and grep can't stitch an HTTP hop to a RabbitMQ hop.`},tech:{stack:[`Serilog`,`Seq`,`.NET`,`ILogger<T>`,`MassTransit`,`RabbitMQ`],note:`Serilog turns log arguments into structured properties and ships them to Seq via its sink, while application code only touches the built-in ILogger<T>. MassTransit over RabbitMQ carries the order events whose cross-service hops need trace correlation.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why grep across three services fails`,body:[`So here's the scene: a purchase order hits the Stocks API, gets saved, gets published to RabbitMQ, the consumer prices it against a third-party API, and — maybe — an OrderFilled event lands in Risk Management and Reporting. Three services, one story. When something goes wrong, your instinct is to grep for the order ID. And that's exactly the trap.`,`Because if each service wrote the order ID inside an interpolated string — 'Processed purchase order for 4f2a...' — Seq receives one opaque text blob. You can't filter on it, you can't join the HTTP hop to the message hop, and you're back to eyeballing three files at 3 AM. The naive fix — write more logs — actually makes the noise problem worse.`,`The real fix is to make every log argument a first-class property. That's the entire pitch of structured logging, and it's the difference between 'text with numbers in it' and 'data you can query'.`],quote:`If you can't filter by it, you can't find it at 3 AM.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Grep three log files`,`Zero setup`,`No cross-service join, pure eyeballing`,`Never`],[`Interpolated strings`,`Reads naturally in the console`,`Zero queryable properties in Seq`,`Never`],[`Message templates + Seq`,`Every argument becomes a filter`,`Needs the sink and some discipline`,`Default choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Message templates and the @ sign that changes everything`,body:[`Let's write it properly. Instead of interpolation you write a message template: LogInformation("Processed purchase order for {OrderId}", orderId). The parameter name becomes the property name, the argument becomes the value. It looks almost identical in code — and radically different in Seq.`,`And here's my favorite trick: when you're holding the whole order object, don't log just the ID — log {@Order}. The @ sign tells Serilog to destructure the object into JSON properties instead of calling ToString(). Put {LastPrice} next to it and suddenly 'couldn't process the purchase order' is queryable by any field on the order.`,`One more thing people miss: the generic argument in ILogger<PurchaseOrderSentConsumer> flows into the logs as the SourceContext property. That's how you filter Seq down to 'everything this one consumer emitted' without writing a single extra line of code.`],quote:`The template is the schema; the arguments are the data.`,table:{headers:[`Log style`,`What Seq receives`,`Queryable?`,`Cost`],rows:[[`"Processed {OrderId}"`,`OrderId as a string property`,`Yes`,`One argument`],[`{@Order} destructured`,`Full order as JSON properties`,`Yes, per field`,`Slightly larger payload`],[`Hardcode id in the string`,`One opaque text blob`,`No`,`Free — and worthless`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,at:`2:40`}},{heading:`03 · Depending on ILogger, not on Serilog`,body:[`Now notice what we are NOT doing: we're not referencing Serilog inside our classes. Application code depends only on the built-in ILogger<T> from Microsoft.Extensions.Logging. Serilog stays an implementation detail you install at the composition root.`,`The wiring is two packages — Serilog.AspNetCore and Serilog.Sinks.Seq — and one line: builder.Host.UseSerilog(...). From there, Serilog formats the structured events and ships them to Seq. And because Datalust maintains both Serilog and Seq, the sink protocol and the query experience are built for each other — that synergy is the honest reason I pick this pair.`,`Why not call Serilog's static Log class everywhere? Because then every class and every unit test is married to Serilog, and swapping sinks becomes a codebase-wide refactor. Keep the abstraction in your hands, the implementation in one file.`],quote:`Inject the abstraction, install the implementation.`,table:{headers:[`Platform`,`Setup cost`,`Query model`,`Fit here`],rows:[[`Seq`,`One sink package`,`Filter on properties, SQL-like queries`,`Best — same vendor as Serilog`],[`ELK stack`,`Cluster + index mapping`,`Kibana DSL`,`Overkill for a three-service system`],[`Application Insights`,`Azure wiring`,`KQL`,`Good, but drags in Azure`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,at:`3:23`}},{heading:`04 · Walking the boundary: HTTP hops and RabbitMQ hops`,body:[`Structured properties solve 'what happened inside this service'. But our order's story crosses boundaries: HTTP in, an outbound HTTP call to the third-party pricing API, and a MassTransit publish over RabbitMQ. Each hop is a different process writing to a different log stream.`,`This is where distributed tracing earns its name. Trace context propagates through headers — W3C traceparent on HTTP, message headers on the bus — so every event, in every service, carries the same trace ID. In Seq you pull up one trace and the whole lifecycle unfolds: the POST, the save, the publish, the pricing call, the OrderFilled fan-out to Risk Management and Reporting.`,`Could you just pass the OrderId everywhere manually? You could — and you should keep logging it as a business key. But the trace ID is automatic, and it covers the hops where no order exists yet, like the pricing API timing out before anything was ever saved.`],quote:`A trace ID is the only witness that rides every hop.`,table:{headers:[`Correlation key`,`Coverage`,`Effort`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Manual OrderId`,`Only hops that know the order`,`You pass it by hand everywhere`,`Keep it as the business key`],[`W3C traceparent`,`Every HTTP and bus hop, automatic`,`Near zero with instrumentation`,`Primary correlation`],[`Both together`,`Trace for hops, OrderId for business queries`,`Trivial`,`Do this`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,at:`4:41`}},{heading:`05 · Why Seq earns its keep`,body:[`So why Seq and not a full ELK cluster? For a system like this — Stocks API as the entry point, in-memory persistence, MassTransit on RabbitMQ, two downstream consumers — Seq gives you property-level filtering, SQL-ish queries, and per-trace drill-down with roughly one afternoon of setup.`,`The workflow I actually use: filter SourceContext down to the consumer that misbehaved, then filter by OrderId or trace ID to get the full story, then use log levels to separate signal from noise. That's the entire debugging loop — and it's all queries, not grep.`],quote:`Logs are only worth what you can query out of them.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,at:`8:27`}}],diagrams:[{id:`order-pipeline`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The three-service order pipeline and its log sinks`,caption:`Every service ships structured events to Seq; the order story crosses HTTP and RabbitMQ boundaries.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Client[Client] -->|"POST /purchase-orders"| Stocks[Stocks API]
    Stocks -->|Save order| MemDB[(In-memory DB)]
    Stocks -->|"Publish PurchaseOrderSent"| Bus[RabbitMQ]
    Bus -->|Consume| Consumer["PurchaseOrderSent Consumer"]
    Consumer -->|"HTTP: fetch last price"| Pricing["Third-party Pricing API"]
    Consumer -->|"Publish OrderFilled"| Bus
    Bus -->|Consume| Risk["Risk Management API"]
    Bus -->|Consume| Reporting["Reporting API"]
    Stocks -.->|"Serilog sink"| Seq[(Seq)]
    Consumer -.->|"Serilog sink"| Seq
    Risk -.->|"Serilog sink"| Seq
    Reporting -.->|"Serilog sink"| Seq`},{id:`order-trace`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One order's lifecycle, one trace`,caption:`Trace context rides HTTP headers and MassTransit message headers, so Seq can stitch every hop into a single trace.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Stocks API
    participant Q as RabbitMQ
    participant P as PurchaseOrderSent Consumer
    participant X as Pricing API
    participant R as Risk Mgmt + Reporting
    participant L as Seq
    C->>S: POST /purchase-orders
    Note over S: traceId born here, log carries {OrderId}
    S->>L: structured event
    S->>Q: PurchaseOrderSent, trace context in headers
    Q->>P: deliver
    P->>X: GET last price
    X-->>P: price
    alt price within limit
        P->>Q: OrderFilled
        Q->>R: delivered to both consumers
        R->>L: events tagged with same traceId
    else price above limit
        P->>L: Couldn't process {@Order} at {LastPrice}
    end`}],takeaways:[`Write message templates, never interpolation: LogInformation("Processed {OrderId}", id) yields one filterable property per argument — an interpolated string yields zero.`,`Destructure complex objects with {@Order} — a single argument turns the entire order into JSON properties you can filter in Seq.`,`Depend on ILogger<T> in code and wire Serilog once with builder.Host.UseSerilog(...) — swapping sinks touches one file, not every class.`,`Ship to Seq via Serilog.Sinks.Seq and filter by SourceContext plus trace ID — one query returns the full order lifecycle across all three services.`,`Propagate correlation across both boundaries — W3C traceparent on HTTP, MassTransit headers on RabbitMQ — so hops without a business key still join the trace.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Mastering Distributed Tracing with Serilog and Seq in .NET`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=F7nnCsIsVi0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-distributed-systems`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-WQFx2m5Ub9M`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:3,title:`Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:42`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Redis`,`EF Core`,`Docker`,`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`The demo that died at dotnet run`,paragraphs:[`It's 9:47 AM on demo day. I clone the products API onto the conference-room laptop, hit F5, and it compiles clean. First request lands — GET /products — and the screen lights up red. 500s, everywhere. The API was never connected to a database; on my machine I'd installed Postgres eight months ago and completely forgotten it existed.`,`My first instinct was the worst one: 'just install Postgres on this laptop too.' Twenty minutes of installers and a half-configured Redis later, a colleague leaned over and asked the question that changed my week: 'Why isn't the database just part of the app's definition?' That afternoon I deleted every manual install and wired the whole system — API, PostgreSQL, Redis — into a single docker-compose.yml.`,`By 5 PM, anyone on the team could go from a fresh clone to a fully working products API — database, cache, CRUD endpoints, the lot — with one command. The morning version of me had been hand-assembling an environment that should have been a file in the repo.`],problem:`A finished .NET 8 API with no reproducible environment: the database and cache existed only as undocumented local installs, so every new machine was a fresh outage.`},tech:{stack:[`Docker Compose`,`.NET 8`,`PostgreSQL`,`Redis`,`EF Core`,`Minimal APIs`,`Multi-stage Dockerfile`,`YAML Overrides`],note:`Compose is the wiring diagram: one YAML file declares the API container, the Postgres database, and the Redis cache on a shared network. The multi-stage Dockerfile turns source code into a lean runtime image that Compose can launch and scale.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'just install Postgres locally' fails`,body:[`Here's where we start: the products API is actually done. Minimal API endpoints in a ProductEndpoints class, EF Core doing honest CRUD — a POST to create, GETs to list and fetch, PUT and DELETE to finish the set. The code is fine. It's dead on arrival anyway, because there's no database behind it.`,`The reflex is to install the missing pieces on whatever machine you're standing at. And that works — right up until the next laptop, the next intern, the next CI agent. The environment isn't in the repo, it's in someone's head, and heads don't clone well. Docker Compose flips this: the database becomes a declared service, versioned next to the code. Visual Studio even scaffolds it for you — right-click the project, Add, Container Orchestrator Support, pick Docker Compose, target Linux.`,`So the question isn't 'how do I install Postgres faster?' It's 'how do I make the environment a build artifact?' That reframing is the whole talk.`],quote:`If your environment lives in someone's head, your uptime lives in their calendar.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Local installs`,`Zero new tooling`,`Undocumented, machine-specific, drifts over time`,`Never — this is the 9:47 AM failure`],[`Docker Compose`,`One YAML, one command, env-as-code`,`Single-host only, no self-healing`,`Local dev and small deployments — the sweet spot`],[`Kubernetes`,`Scaling, self-healing, prod-grade`,`Heavy setup, overkill for a three-service API`,`Later, when Compose genuinely runs out of road`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQFx2m5Ub9M`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the Dockerfile is a build contract`,body:[`Before Compose can run anything, the Dockerfile has to describe how source becomes an image. And the pattern here is the one you should internalize: multi-stage. Stage one pulls the .NET 8 SDK — the fat image with the compilers — and builds and publishes the project. Stage two starts fresh from the aspnet:8 runtime image and copies in only the published output.`,`Why not just ship the SDK image? Because you'd be dragging compilers into production for zero benefit: bigger pull, bigger attack surface, slower cold starts. Build with everything, ship with almost nothing. And here's the kicker — an image is a template. If you can spin up one container, you can spin up ten. That's your horizontal scaling story, and it only works if the image is small and identical every time.`,`The last piece of the contract is ports. I explicitly expose 5000 for HTTP and 5001 for HTTPS in the Dockerfile, so the container's promise matches what Compose will map. Get sloppy here and you'll spend an afternoon debugging 'connection refused' that was really 'port never declared.'`],quote:`Build with everything, ship with almost nothing.`,table:{headers:[`Base Image`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`aspnet:8 runtime`,`~110 MB, minimal surface, fast pull`,`No build tools — can't compile inside it`,`Final stage, always`],[`sdk:8`,`Full build + publish capability`,`~1 GB+, ships compilers to prod`,`Build stage only`],[`Alpine variants`,`Smallest footprint`,`Musl libc quirks with some .NET workloads`,`Only if image size is a hard constraint`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQFx2m5Ub9M`,at:`1:01`}},{heading:`03 · Compose is the wiring diagram, not just a launcher`,body:[`Once the scaffold exists, the docker-compose.yml becomes the single file that decides which services run when you hit start. The generated entry points at the Dockerfile inside the products-api project — Compose doesn't replace your build, it orchestrates it. Then I map ports explicitly: container 5000 to host 5000, container 5001 to host 5001, so my browser and Postman hit the same addresses the container serves.`,`Now, the override file — this is the part people skip and regret. Visual Studio generates docker-compose.override.yml alongside the base file, and Compose loads both automatically. Environment-specific things live there: the port mappings, the ASPNETCORE_URLS, the HTTP and HTTPS environment variables. The base YAML stays the clean, shared contract; the override is where each developer's local negotiation happens without forking the team config.`,`The mental model I want you to leave with: Compose isn't a launcher, it's a wiring diagram. Every service, port mapping, and environment variable is an edge in the graph, drawn in a file you can diff, review, and version.`],quote:`The base YAML is the contract; the override file is the negotiation.`,table:{headers:[`Config Location`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`docker-compose.yml`,`Shared, versioned, reviewable`,`Wrong place for machine-specific values`,`Service definitions, shared defaults`],[`Override file`,`Auto-loaded, per-dev tweaks, no forking`,`Silently applied — know it exists`,`Ports, local env vars, debug flags`],[`appsettings.json`,`Familiar, typed, per-environment files`,`Rebaking the image to change config`,`App-level settings, not infrastructure`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQFx2m5Ub9M`,at:`2:07`}},{heading:`04 · Database and cache as first-class services`,body:[`Adding the database is almost anticlimactic: declare a service called products-database, point it at the postgres image from hub.docker.com, and Compose pulls it and puts it on the same network as the API. Same move for Redis. Two YAML blocks and the API's missing organs are back.`,`But here's the design question worth pausing on: why an external Redis instead of in-memory caching inside the API? Because of that horizontal-scaling promise from block two. The moment you run two API containers, in-process caches diverge — instance A caches a product, instance B serves the stale one. One shared Redis means N containers, one cache, consistent answers. The cache has to outlive any single container, so it becomes its own service.`,`And the connection strings? Inside the Compose network, service names are DNS names. Your EF Core connection string says products-database, not localhost — that's the wiring paying off. One honest caveat: the tutorial reaches for postgres:latest, and 'latest' is a different database every quarter. Pin the major version in anything you'll keep.`],quote:`Service names in Compose are DNS names — your connection string should say 'products-database', not 'localhost'.`,table:{headers:[`Caching Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`No cache`,`Simplest, always fresh`,`Every request hits Postgres`,`Prototypes only`],[`In-memory (per instance)`,`Zero latency, no extra service`,`Diverges across replicas, lost on restart`,`Single-instance apps only`],[`Redis sidecar service`,`Shared across N replicas, survives restarts`,`One more container to run and monitor`,`Any horizontally scaled API — this talk's choice`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQFx2m5Ub9M`,at:`3:41`}}],diagrams:[{id:`compose-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One YAML, three services: the Compose topology`,caption:`docker-compose.yml declares all three services on a shared network; service names double as DNS names for the connection strings.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    dev["Developer"] -->|"docker compose up"| compose["docker-compose.yml\\n+ override"]
    compose -.->|"defines"| api
    compose -.->|"defines"| db
    compose -.->|"defines"| cache
    client["HTTP Client"] -->|"GET /products\\nports 5000 / 5001"| api["products-api\\n.NET 8 container"]
    api -->|"EF Core\\nport 5432"| db["products-database\\npostgres image"]
    api -->|"IDistributedCache\\nport 6379"| cache["redis-cache\\nredis image"]`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Request path: cache-aside through the wired stack`,caption:`A GET hits Redis first; only on a miss does EF Core touch Postgres — and the answer is written back so the next replica benefits.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant C as Client
    participant A as products-api (.NET 8)
    participant R as Redis :6379
    participant P as Postgres :5432
    C->>A: GET /products/42
    A->>R: GET product:42
    alt cache hit
        R-->>A: cached JSON
    else cache miss
        A->>P: SELECT via EF Core
        P-->>A: product row
        A->>R: SET product:42
    end
    A-->>C: 200 OK`}],takeaways:[`Multi-stage builds cut the shipped image from roughly 1 GB (SDK) to about 110 MB (aspnet:8 runtime) — build fat, ship thin, and horizontal scaling gets cheap.`,`Pin image tags: postgres:latest is a different database every quarter; postgres:16 is a promise. Pin major versions in any compose file that outlives the demo.`,`Declare ports in both layers — EXPOSE 5000;5001 in the Dockerfile and 5000:5000 mappings in compose — or budget an hour of 'connection refused' debugging.`,`Put machine-specific config in docker-compose.override.yml (auto-loaded by Compose) and keep the base YAML as the reviewed, shared contract.`,`One-command cold start: a fresh clone should reach a working API + Postgres + Redis in under 2 minutes with docker compose up — if it can't, the environment still lives in someone's head.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Docker Compose with .NET 8, PostgreSQL, and Redis (step by step)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQFx2m5Ub9M`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-deployment`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-SI38jyEiso4`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:4,title:`Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:32`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Docker`,`Azure`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The $0 Deploy That Shipped Green and Served Nothing`,paragraphs:["It's 11 PM on a side-project Friday, and locally everything is boring in the best possible way. `docker compose up` brings a Postgres online, the ASP.NET Core 9 API serves CRUD for a Products resource — a fairly complicated model with nested types — and `npm run dev` renders the Next.js catalog on localhost:3000. Three products, paging works, create and delete work. Time to push this whole thing to the cloud. For free.","The Static Web App deploys in minutes. `swa deploy` zips the out folder, uploads it with the deployment token, and hands back a shiny HTTPS URL. I click it. Padlock icon. Beautiful UI. Zero products. Every single data call fails — because the API doesn't exist in the cloud yet, and the static export had baked the API URL into the JavaScript bundle at build time. The frontend was 'successfully deployed' to a URL with nothing to talk to.",`That's the free-tier trap in one screenshot: three services that each deploy independently, joined by a build-time contract nobody warned me about. The deploy was green. The system wasn't. Here's how the pieces actually fit together — and the order you have to do them in.`],problem:`A green deploy is not a working system: the statically exported Next.js site went live with the API URL baked in at build time, pointing at an API that hadn't been deployed yet — so every product request failed on a 'successful' release.`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core 9`,`Next.js Static Export`,`Azure Static Web Apps`,`Azure App Service`,`SWA CLI`,`Managed Postgres`,`Docker Compose`,`Deployment Token`],note:`Three free-tier hosts play three roles: Azure Static Web Apps serves the statically exported Next.js client, Azure App Service runs the ASP.NET Core 9 API, and a separate managed free-tier Postgres holds the data — wired together by one API URL env var and one connection string. Locally, Docker Compose stands in for the database.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: can a whole stack really cost $0?`,body:[`Let's inventory what we're actually hosting: a React client, a .NET 9 Web API with CRUD endpoints, and a Postgres database. The question isn't 'can Azure host this' — it's 'which free tier fits each piece'. The client goes to Azure Static Web Apps on the free plan. The API goes to Azure App Service. And the database? Azure doesn't really give Postgres away at $0, so it lives with a separate managed provider's free tier.`,`Why not one VM running everything? Because a VM is never free, and the moment you have one, you're its sysadmin — patching, restarting, on-call for a portfolio app. Why not containers everywhere? Same reason: the free tiers don't cover the orchestration. The trade you're making is limits and shared compute in exchange for zero ops and zero bill, and for a small project that's exactly the right trade.`],quote:`Free tiers aren't a discount on architecture — they're a constraint that picks your architecture for you.`,table:{headers:[`Piece`,`Free home`,`Catch`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Next.js client`,`Azure Static Web Apps (free plan)`,`Must be a static export`,`Perfect`],[`.NET 9 API`,`Azure App Service (free tier)`,`Shared compute, resource limits`,`Good`],[`Postgres`,`External managed free tier`,`Storage limits, second vendor`,`Good`],[`One VM for all`,`Nothing free exists`,`You patch it at 3 AM`,`Skip`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The Next.js trap: it's a server until you say it isn't`,body:["Here's the gotcha that bites everyone: Next.js by default wants a Node server — SSR, API routes, middleware, the works. Static Web Apps on the free plan serves files. Those two worldviews do not overlap. The fix is two lines: set `output: 'export'` in next.config so the build emits a fully static site, and point the SWA config's output location at the `out` folder instead of the default.",`What do you give up? Server rendering, runtime API routes, image optimization on the server. For a catalog UI that just fetches from the .NET API over HTTP — nothing that matters. Why not keep SSR and pay for a plan that supports it? Because the entire point of this exercise is $0, and this app genuinely doesn't need a server. Azure Static Web Apps does support Next.js more broadly, but the free static path is the honest, simple one.`],quote:`If your frontend doesn't need a server, stop paying for one — even when the price is zero, know what you're paying for.`,table:{headers:[`Choice`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Static export`,`Free hosting, CDN-fast, trivial deploys`,`No SSR, no API routes, env vars baked at build`,`This app: yes`],[`SSR on paid plan`,`Full Next.js feature set`,`Costs money, needs a Node host`,`Overkill here`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,at:`2:06`}},{heading:`03 · Deploying by hand: three commands and a token`,body:["Azure will happily wire GitHub Actions or Azure DevOps for you — connect the repo and it scaffolds a CI/CD pipeline. I deliberately skipped all of that. Instead: `swa init` detects the Next.js app and writes a config, `swa build` produces the out folder, and `swa deploy --env production` with the deployment token copied from the portal's 'Manage deployment token' button zips everything up and uploads it.",`Why manual? Not because CI is bad — because the first deploy should be legible. Doing it by hand teaches you what the pipeline would have hidden: there's a deployment token that is the only credential the CLI needs, and by default you land in a preview environment where you can review before promoting to production. Production is an explicit choice, not an accident. Automate the second deploy; understand the first one with your hands.`],quote:`Automate the second deploy. Understand the first one with your hands.`,table:{headers:[`Path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`SWA CLI manual`,`Zero setup, full visibility, token is the only secret`,`You retype it every release`,`First deploy`],[`GitHub Actions`,`Push-to-deploy, preview envs per PR`,`YAML to maintain, harder to debug blind`,`Once it works`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,at:`4:40`}},{heading:`04 · Giving the API a home — and a URL that actually exists`,body:["App Service is the boring, correct answer for the ASP.NET Core 9 API: same resource group as the static site, free tier, and it hands you an HTTPS endpoint with essentially zero configuration. The subtle part is the wiring. The static export baked the API URL into the bundle from an environment variable at build time — so that variable must be set before `swa build`, not after. Change the URL, and you rebuild and redeploy.",`And this is exactly where my Friday night fell apart: the site deployed first, the API didn't exist yet, and every call failed against a URL pointing at nothing. Order of operations is a feature of the architecture, not a detail. The next thing you'll meet is CORS — the API must explicitly allow the Static Web Apps origin, or the browser will silently eat every response.`],quote:`In a static frontend, configuration isn't runtime — it's compile time. Set the URL before you build, or rebuild.`,table:{headers:[`Mistake`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`Site deployed before API`,`UI loads, every data call fails`,`Deploy the API first — or expect red until it's up`],[`API URL set after build`,`Old or localhost URL baked into the bundle`,`Set env var, rebuild, redeploy`],[`CORS not configured`,`Browser blocks responses, console errors`,`Allow the SWA origin in the API`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,at:`6:40`}},{heading:`05 · The database: same Postgres, different zip code`,body:[`Locally it's Docker Compose — a throwaway Postgres with seed data, up in seconds, gone when I say so. In the cloud I don't want to run Postgres myself, so it's a managed free tier from a separate provider. Their dashboard has a connect button where you choose how you want to connect, and it hands you a connection string. That string becomes the API's single environment variable in App Service configuration.`,`The beautiful part: the API code doesn't change at all. Postgres is Postgres — the driver doesn't care what zip code it lives in. The discipline part: that connection string is a secret, it lives in App Service configuration, and it never touches the repo. One connection string is the entire difference between localhost and the cloud. Guard it like it's the whole system, because functionally, it is.`],quote:`One connection string is the entire difference between localhost and the cloud — guard it like it's the whole system, because it is.`,table:{headers:[`Setup`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Docker Compose locally`,`Fast, disposable, seeded data`,`Unreachable from the cloud`,`Dev only`],[`Managed free Postgres`,`Zero ops, public endpoint, TLS`,`Limits, second vendor`,`Cloud: yes`],[`Self-hosted PG on a VM`,`Full control`,`No free VM, you're on-call`,`Skip`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,at:`8:03`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two worlds, one codebase: local dev vs the $0 cloud topology`,caption:`Locally, Docker Compose plays Postgres and everything runs on localhost. In the cloud, three free-tier services each own one layer, joined by an API URL and a connection string.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  Browser["Browser"]
  subgraph Local["Local dev machine"]
    direction LR
    N["Next.js dev server :3000"] --> A["ASP.NET Core 9 API"]
    A --> P["Postgres via Docker Compose"]
  end
  subgraph Cloud["Cloud — $0 per month"]
    direction LR
    S["Azure Static Web Apps — static export out folder"] -->|"HTTPS fetch — API URL baked at build"| W["Azure App Service — .NET 9 API"]
    W -->|"connection string"| G["Managed Postgres — free tier"]
  end
  Browser --> S`},{id:`deploy-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The deploy sequence — including the green-but-broken moment`,caption:`The exact order that avoids the 11 PM failure: build with the API URL set, deploy the API before celebrating the frontend, then the full request path goes live.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant D as Developer
  participant C as SWA CLI
  participant S as Static Web Apps
  participant A as App Service API
  participant P as Postgres
  D->>C: swa init, then swa build
  D->>S: Copy deployment token from portal
  D->>C: swa deploy out --env production
  C->>S: Zip and upload, token auth
  S-->>D: Live HTTPS URL
  Note over D,A: Green deploy — but API not deployed yet, every data call fails
  D->>A: Deploy .NET 9 API, set connection string
  D->>C: Rebuild with API URL env var, redeploy
  Note over D,P: Full path now live
  D->>S: Open site
  S-->>D: Static HTML and JS bundle
  D->>A: GET /api/products
  A->>P: SELECT products
  P-->>A: Rows
  A-->>D: JSON — UI renders catalog`}],takeaways:[`Plan for exactly three free resources — Static Web Apps for the client, App Service for the API, a managed free-tier Postgres — because a 'full-stack deploy' is three deploys plus two wires, not one.`,"Two config lines unlock free static hosting: `output: 'export'` in next.config and `out` as the SWA output location — a default SSR Next.js build will not run on the free static plan.","Three CLI commands ship the frontend — `swa init`, `swa build`, `swa deploy --env production` — with the portal's deployment token as the only credential the CLI needs.","Set the API URL environment variable before `swa build`: static exports bake config at build time, so a changed URL means a rebuild — and deploy the API before the client, or the green site serves errors.",`Keep exactly one difference between environments — the Postgres connection string (Docker Compose locally, managed free tier in the cloud) — stored in App Service configuration, never in git.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Deploy Full-Stack .NET Apps for FREE in 2025 (Azure + Postgres)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SI38jyEiso4`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-deployment`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-SA8BainHeS0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:5,title:`How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 13:28`,minutes:13,difficulty:3,tags:[`EF Core`,`Jaeger`,`Docker`],story:{headline:`The Article View That Took 40 Seconds — and Left No Fingerprints`,paragraphs:[`It's 11:45 when batch reconciliation started. A dashboard alert says p95 latency on 'article views' just tripled. You open the logs for the Newsletter API — the GET request is right there, 12ms, perfectly healthy. But the user swears the page took forever. You grep the Reporting API logs and find the 'article.viewed' consumer firing... 40 seconds after the request. Two services, two log streams, zero shared identifiers. Which one is lying?`,`You spend the next hour eyeballing timestamps and matching GUIDs by hand, mentally reconstructing the request path from stack traces. The culprit turns out to be a slow SQL write in the reporting service — a fact you could have read off a single waterfall chart in ten seconds if both services had been emitting spans into the same trace.`,`That night we wired OpenTelemetry into both services and pointed them at a Jaeger all-in-one container. The next incident, the entire story — HTTP request, EF Core query, broker publish, consumer, database write — was one trace, seven spans, one screen. Nobody grepped anything at 3 AM again.`],problem:`A single user action crosses two APIs, a message broker, and two databases — but the logs in each service have no way to prove they belong to the same story.`},tech:{stack:[`OpenTelemetry`,`Jaeger All-in-One`,`EF Core`,`SQL Server`,`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`Docker Compose`,`OTLP gRPC`],note:`OpenTelemetry SDKs instrument the two .NET services at every boundary (HTTP, SQL, MassTransit) and ship spans over OTLP to a Jaeger all-in-one container, which stitches cross-service and cross-broker activity into a single trace.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why can't I just grep harder?`,body:[`Let's start where everyone starts: logs. You have three kinds of telemetry in the OpenTelemetry world — traces, metrics, and logs — and they answer different questions. Metrics tell you something is wrong and how much. Logs tell you what happened right here, in this one process. But neither one tells you the causal story of a request that hopped through five components.`,`The classic counter-argument is 'we'll just inject a correlation ID into every log line.' Fine — until you realize you're now hand-rolling context propagation across HTTP calls, message brokers, and background jobs, forever, in every service. OpenTelemetry exists precisely so you don't reinvent that wheel: it's a vendor-neutral standard for generating telemetry, and tracing is where it pays back first.`,`So my opening move in any microservices codebase is traces. They're the only telemetry type that automatically links every hop — including the async ones through RabbitMQ — under one trace ID.`],quote:`Metrics tell you the house is on fire. Logs tell you which room. Traces hand you the floor plan.`,table:{headers:[`Telemetry Type`,`Answers`,`Blind Spot`,`Best First Investment`],rows:[[`Metrics`,`Is something wrong? How much?`,`No causality — can't explain why`,`Second, after traces`],[`Logs`,`What happened in this process?`,`No cross-service linkage by default`,`Third, enrich with trace IDs later`],[`Traces`,`What path did this request take, end to end?`,`Sampling can hide rare paths`,`First — biggest early win`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SA8BainHeS0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: instrument every edge the request touches`,body:[`Look at where a request actually goes in this system: inbound HTTP on the Newsletter API, an EF Core query into SQL Server, a publish out to RabbitMQ, a consume on the Reporting API, and another database write. Each of those boundaries has a ready-made instrumentation package — AspNetCore, HttpClient, SqlClient, MassTransit. You don't write tracing code; you register listeners.`,`The wiring itself is almost boring: AddOpenTelemetry(), ConfigureResource() with AddService() to name the app, then WithTracing() and the instrumentation extensions. That service name is what Jaeger groups traces under — get it right, and duplicate the whole block into the second service changing only the name.`,`One flag deserves a pause: SetDbStatementForText on the SQL client instrumentation. Set it true and every EF Core query's full text shows up in the span — phenomenal in development, a genuine sensitive-data leak in production. Know which environment you're in before you flip it.`],quote:`If a boundary isn't instrumented, your trace has a hole exactly where the bug lives.`,table:{headers:[`Instrumented Edge`,`Package / Hook`,`What You See`,`Prod Caution`],rows:[[`Inbound HTTP`,`Instrumentation.AspNetCore`,`Every request as a root span`,`Low risk`],[`Outbound HTTP`,`Instrumentation.HttpClient`,`Downstream calls with latency`,`Low risk`],[`Database`,`Instrumentation.SqlClient`,`EF Core queries as child spans`,`SetDbStatementForText leaks query text`],[`Message bus`,`AddSource(MassTransit listener)`,`Publish and consume spans`,`Verify header propagation`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SA8BainHeS0`,at:`1:25`}},{heading:`03 · Following the message: the broker is where traces go to die`,body:[`Here's the hop that breaks most people's tracing: RabbitMQ. Over HTTP, context propagation is automatic — the traceparent header rides along and every downstream service joins the same trace. But a message published now and consumed forty seconds later has no HTTP request to piggyback on. The context has to be serialized into the message headers and rehydrated on the consumer side.`,`MassTransit does carry that context, but OpenTelemetry won't listen for it unless you tell it to: AddSource with MassTransit's DiagnosticHeaders.DefaultListenerName. Skip that one line and the consumer starts a brand-new trace — your beautiful waterfall becomes two orphaned fragments, and the async half of your system goes dark.`,`So this is my day-one smoke test: publish a message, open Jaeger, and check that the consume span shares the publisher's trace ID. If it doesn't, nothing downstream of the broker is observable, no matter how much instrumentation you've installed.`],quote:`A trace that stops at the broker is half a story — and the second half is where your latency hides.`,table:{headers:[`Propagation Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`W3C traceparent (HTTP)`,`Automatic, standardized`,`Only covers synchronous hops`,`Default for all HTTP calls`],[`Messaging headers (MassTransit)`,`Links async consumers to publishers`,`Needs explicit AddSource wiring`,`Must-have for any broker`],[`Manual correlation IDs`,`Works absolutely anywhere`,`You maintain it by hand forever`,`Fallback for exotic transports`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SA8BainHeS0`,at:`5:23`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring the backend: Jaeger all-in-one in the compose file`,body:[`Now the services are producing spans — where do they go? Jaeger. It's a distributed tracing platform that maps the flow of requests and data as they traverse the system, and the all-in-one image bundles collector, storage, and UI into a single container. In docker-compose I add it alongside SQL Server and RabbitMQ and expose three ports: 16686 for the UI, 4317 for OTLP over gRPC, 4318 for OTLP over HTTP.`,`The services learn Jaeger's address from the OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT environment variable — which the AddOpenTelemetryExporter call in our setup picks up automatically. That's the architectural payoff of OpenTelemetry: repointing from local Jaeger to a managed collector later is a config change, not a rewrite.`,`Be honest about what all-in-one is: perfect for local development and demos, not a production backend. Its storage is in-memory, so the moment you care about history, you graduate to a real collector-and-storage topology or a SaaS APM.`],quote:`The tracing backend is an environment variable, not a line of code — that's the whole point of OpenTelemetry.`,table:{headers:[`Backend Setup`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Jaeger all-in-one`,`One container, instant UI`,`In-memory storage, not durable`,`Local dev and demos`],[`OTel Collector + durable store`,`Buffering, sampling, retention`,`More moving parts to operate`,`Staging and production`],[`SaaS APM vendor`,`Zero ops, rich dashboards`,`Cost and vendor lock-in`,`Teams without ops bandwidth`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SA8BainHeS0`,at:`10:23`}},{heading:`05 · Reading the waterfall: seven spans, one story`,body:[`Fire a few requests through Swagger, open the Jaeger UI on port 16686, pick the Newsletter API from the services dropdown, and open a GET trace. There it is: one distributed trace, seven spans — the ASP.NET Core request span, the EF Core query under it, the message publish, the consumer on the reporting side, its database write. Time runs left to right; nesting is causality.`,`Reading it is a skill worth ten hours of log archaeology. The widest span is your latency suspect. A gap between the publish span and the consume span is queue lag or consumer backlog. Deep nesting under one span means chatty downstream calls. And orphaned fragments that don't connect mean broken propagation — go fix the headers before you scale anything.`,`This is the moment the system becomes legible. You stop asking 'which service is slow?' and start pointing at a specific rectangle on a timeline.`],quote:`Seven spans on one screen beat two hundred log lines across two dashboards.`,table:{headers:[`Span Pattern`,`What It Usually Means`,`Action`],rows:[[`One wide span, thin children`,`The work inside that span is slow`,`Profile that component directly`],[`Gap between publish and consume`,`Queue lag or consumer backlog`,`Check consumer throughput`],[`Deep nesting under one span`,`Chatty downstream calls`,`Batch, cache, or parallelize`],[`Orphaned trace fragments`,`Broken context propagation`,`Fix headers before scaling`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Two services, one broker, one trace backend`,caption:`Both APIs instrument every boundary and export spans over OTLP; trace context rides through RabbitMQ so the consumer joins the same trace.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    SW["Swagger UI"] --> API["Newsletter API"]
    API --> DB1[("SQL Server")]
    API -- "publish ArticleViewed" --> MQ[("RabbitMQ")]
    MQ -- "consume" --> REP["Newsletter Reporting API"]
    REP --> DB2[("SQL Server")]
    API -. "trace context" .-> MQ
    MQ -. "trace context" .-> REP
    API -- "OTLP spans 4317" --> JG["Jaeger All-in-One"]
    REP -- "OTLP spans 4317" --> JG
    JG --> UI["Jaeger UI 16686"]`},{id:`request-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One GET request, seven spans`,caption:`Sequence of a single article fetch: root span at the API, child spans for SQL and messaging, context injected into the broker and extracted by the consumer, all exported to Jaeger.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant A as Newsletter API
    participant DB as SQL Server
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant R as Reporting API
    participant J as Jaeger
    C->>A: GET /articles/:id
    Note over A: root span created, traceparent injected
    A->>DB: EF Core query (child span)
    A->>MQ: publish ArticleViewed + inject context
    A-->>C: 200 OK
    MQ->>R: deliver message + extract context
    Note over R: same trace ID, consume span attached
    R->>DB: EF Core write (child span)
    A->>J: export spans via OTLP
    R->>J: export spans via OTLP`}],takeaways:[`Instrument four edges per service — ASP.NET Core, HttpClient, SqlClient, and MassTransit — and you capture roughly 90% of all spans in a typical .NET microservice.`,`Set the service name once per app and the backend once per environment via OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT; swapping Jaeger for a collector should cost zero code changes.`,`Keep SetDbStatementForText=true in dev to see every EF Core query in the waterfall, but flip it to false in prod — raw SQL text inside spans is a sensitive-data leak.`,`Verify RabbitMQ context propagation on day one: if a consumer span doesn't share the publisher's trace ID, every async path in your system is invisible no matter what else you instrument.`,`Read traces with three patterns: the widest span is your latency suspect, gaps between spans are queue time, and orphaned trace fragments mean broken propagation — that triage covers most incidents in under a minute.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How To Implement Distributed Tracing for Microservices With Jaeger`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SA8BainHeS0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-distributed-systems`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Qk4F36MbNKg`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:6,title:`I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 27:41`,minutes:28,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`],story:{headline:`3 A.M. with 6,000 Lines of C# and an Agent I Didn't Fully Trust`,paragraphs:[`It started as a dare to myself: every AI coding tool looks immortal in a demo, so I pointed one at the realest thing I had — Evently, a .NET 8 modular monolith with more than 6,000 lines of code. Not a toy repo. The kind of codebase where a bad refactor doesn't fail loudly; it fails quietly, three modules later, when someone finally calls the method the agent 'helpfully' rewrote.`,`The tension was real. I installed the Augment Code extension in VS Code, hit 'index codebase,' and watched the sync like it was a heart monitor. If the context engine was marketing fluff, the agent would hallucinate APIs and steamroll module boundaries — and I'd be untangling that mess at 3 A.M. If it was real, I'd finally have an assistant that knew MY C#, not just C# in general.`,`Then I asked it to summarize a component I knew cold. The answer came back fast, snappy, and — this is the scary part — correct, grounded in the actual code. That's when the fear flipped: not 'will it break my codebase?' but 'how much can I delegate before I stop reading the diffs?'`],problem:`Can an AI agent actually understand a large, production-grade .NET codebase — or does it fall apart the moment the demo ends? And what does trusting it cost, in dollars and in data?`},tech:{stack:[`Augment Code`,`.NET 8`,`VS Code`,`Context Engine`,`Agent Mode`,`Modular Monolith`],note:`The moving parts, in the order the speaker meets them: the AI platform, the editor it plugs into, the indexing layer that makes a 6,000-line monolith legible, and the modes that turn answers into edits.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why demo-grade AI fails at scale`,body:[`Alright, here's my first thought walking in. Every AI coding tool looks great in a demo because the demo repo is, what, two hundred lines? The model has effectively memorized the entire problem space. Drop the same tool into a real .NET project with a few thousand lines and the failure mode is brutal: it doesn't know your interfaces, your conventions, your module boundaries — so it guesses. Confidently.`,`So the real question was never 'can the model write C#?' Of course it can. The question is 'does it know MY C#?' That's the gap Augment Code claims to close with its context engine — an AI platform built specifically for large, production-grade codebases. Full disclosure: this exploration was sponsored by them, but everything I'm telling you is my honest read after actually running it.`,`So my test protocol was simple: no toy tasks. Real questions against a real codebase, judged on one axis — are the answers grounded in the code, or hallucinated?`],quote:`A model that doesn't know your codebase is just a very confident stranger.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Toy-repo demo`,`Looks magical, zero setup`,`Collapses on real conventions`,`Marketing only`],[`Generic copilot`,`Great autocomplete`,`Guesses APIs in big repos`,`Small projects`],[`Context-engine AI`,`Grounded in your actual code`,`Requires an indexing step`,`Large codebases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qk4F36MbNKg`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: the context engine`,body:[`Here's the part I wanted to stress-test: the context engine. The workflow is dead simple — install the extension from the VS Code marketplace, authenticate, and the very first thing you do is click 'index codebase.' That kicks off a sync, and depending on the size of your codebase it may take some time. Mine finished quickly and I got the 'Augment just finished syncing with your codebase' message.`,`Why does indexing matter? Because the alternative is prompt stuffing — pasting files into a chat window and praying they fit. At 6,000+ lines you can't fit the world in a prompt, and even if you could, the model would drown in irrelevant context. The index flips it: every question gets answered against a searchable snapshot of your actual code, which is why the feedback felt fast and snappy no matter what I asked.`,`And here's the pro move: the first thing you do after indexing isn't a refactor — it's a summary. Ask it to explain a component you know cold. If the summary is right, the retrieval is right. If the summary is wrong, you just saved yourself from a very bad edit.`],quote:`Index first, ask second — retrieval is the whole game at scale.`,table:{headers:[`Context Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Prompt stuffing`,`No setup required`,`Blows up past a few files`,`Tiny repos`],[`No context at all`,`Zero cost`,`Pure hallucination`,`Never`],[`Indexed retrieval`,`Fast, grounded answers`,`Indexing step and sync wait`,`6k+ LOC codebases`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qk4F36MbNKg`,at:`0:44`}},{heading:`03 · Trust, but diff: agent mode inside a monolith`,body:[`Now the scary part — letting it touch code. The surface will feel familiar if you've used Copilot or Cursor: a side panel, a chat window, inline edits inside your source files, and full agent mode. Familiar is good; it means the learning curve is basically zero.`,`But here's my rule for a modular monolith: the module boundaries ARE the architecture. An agent that 'helpfully' reaches across them creates coupling you'll pay for years. So the workflow that earned my trust was graduated — summaries first, then scoped inline edits, then agent mode for the bigger sweep, reviewing every diff like it was a junior dev's pull request.`,`And honestly, that's the mental shift. The agent isn't a magic refactor button; it's a very fast pair programmer whose work you still review. Trust is earned per-diff, not per-demo.`],quote:`Let the agent drive — but keep your hand on the diff.`,table:{headers:[`Mode`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Chat Q&A`,`Zero risk, great for summaries`,`Doesn't change code`,`Onboarding and learning`],[`Inline edits`,`Scoped, easy to review`,`Manual, per-file`,`Targeted changes`],[`Agent mode`,`Multi-file sweeps`,`Demands strict diff review`,`Big refactors`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qk4F36MbNKg`,at:`2:52`}},{heading:`04 · The price of admission: dollars and data`,body:[`Last thing, and it's the one nobody puts in the demo: the price of admission. The free plan gives you up to 50 user messages a month — enough for a smoke test, not a workflow. But read the fine print: on the free tier, you're agreeing to allow AI training on your data.`,`The paid plans start at $50 a month, and there, no AI training is allowed on your code. That single clause is the entire decision for enterprise codebases. For a side project, 50 free messages might be fine. For a company with proprietary source, the free tier is paying with the one asset you can never get back.`,`So that's actually why I think Augment is worth considering for large enterprise codebases — the security posture isn't a footnote, it's the headline. Fifty dollars a month to keep your source out of a training set is, frankly, cheap insurance.`],quote:`On the free tier, your code is the payment.`,table:{headers:[`Plan`,`Messages`,`AI Training on Your Data`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Free`,`Up to 50 / month`,`Allowed`,`Smoke tests only`],[`Paid (from $50/mo)`,`Paid message quota`,`Not allowed`,`Enterprise / proprietary code`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qk4F36MbNKg`,at:`3:27`}}],diagrams:[{id:`context-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The Index-Then-Ask Loop`,caption:`Index once, then every question and patch flows through the context engine before a human ever sees a diff.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    Dev["Developer"] -->|"task / question"| Ext["VS Code Extension"]
    Ext --> Agent["Augment Agent Mode"]
    Agent --> CE["Context Engine"]
    CE --> Idx[("Indexed Snapshot")]
    Repo["Evently Monolith<br/>.NET 8 · 6,000+ LOC"] -->|"index & sync"| Idx
    CE -->|"grounded context"| Agent
    Agent -->|"proposed patch"| Diff["Inline Diff Review"]
    Diff -->|"accept / reject"| Repo`},{id:`request-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One Refactor Request, End to End`,caption:`Note the key detail: the model never touches the code directly — every answer is routed through the index first.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant D as Developer
    participant E as VS Code Extension
    participant A as Augment Agent
    participant C as Context Engine
    participant R as Evently Repo
    D->>E: Summarize or refactor component X
    E->>A: Forward task
    A->>C: Retrieve relevant context
    C->>R: Query indexed snapshot
    R-->>C: Matched symbols and modules
    C-->>A: Grounded context
    A-->>E: Summary or proposed edit
    E-->>D: Inline diff for review
    D->>E: Accept or reject
    E->>R: Apply accepted changes`}],takeaways:[`Index before you ask: click 'index codebase' and wait for the sync to complete — grounded answers only start after indexing finishes, not before.`,`Treat the free tier as a smoke test: 50 user messages per month is enough to validate the context engine on a 6,000+ line repo, not to run a full refactor.`,`Price the data clause: paid plans start at $50/month and disable AI training on your code — on the free tier, your source code is the payment.`,`Graduate trust in stages: summaries → inline edits → agent mode; if a summary of code you know cold comes back wrong, don't let it edit anything.`,`Review every diff like a junior dev's PR: in a modular monolith, one accepted bad hunk can couple modules for years — accept per-hunk, never wholesale.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Used AI to Refactor a Massive .NET App. It Was Scary Good`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qk4F36MbNKg`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`ai-coding-workflow`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-o_KVvUjwxIw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:7,title:`C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:53`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`C#`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The 87% coverage suite that let a double-follow ship`,paragraphs:[`It's 23:40 and a support ticket lands in the incident channel: users are following the same account twice, follower counts are inflating, and the 'already following' guard in FollowerService is clearly not firing. The twist? CI is green. The coverage report proudly says 87%. The tests for StartFollowingAsync exist — they've existed for months.`,`I pull them up and find the real problem: they aren't unit tests. Every so-called unit test was bootstrapping a real database, so the suite took eleven minutes, flaked whenever two branches raced against the same seed rows, and got skipped locally because nobody wanted to wait. The 'already following' branch had a test that only passed when the seed data was in the right mood — and tonight, it wasn't.`,`The rewrite took an afternoon: xUnit facts, NSubstitute stubs, no database anywhere. Three validation branches became three deterministic tests, each named after the exact domain error it asserts, each running in milliseconds. The double-follow bug had a regression test within the hour — and this time, the test was actually about the code.`],problem:`How do you test every validation branch of StartFollowingAsync — same user, non-public profile, already following — without dragging a database or any external dependency into the test?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET`,`xUnit`,`NSubstitute`,`Domain Errors`],note:`The unit under test is FollowerService and its single public method, StartFollowingAsync. xUnit hosts the facts, NSubstitute stubs the IFollowerRepository seam, and the typed domain errors in FollowerErrors become the assertions.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: what is the unit, really?`,body:[`So let's set the stage. The unit here is the FollowerService class — and honestly, I don't even care about the whole class. I care about the one public method it exposes: StartFollowingAsync. That's the contract the rest of the system actually touches, so that's where the tests earn their keep.`,`Now open that method and look at its shape. There are a handful of branches, and every single one is a validation: are you trying to follow yourself? Is the target's profile public? Are you already following them? Those branches are the whole point. If a branch can produce a different outcome, it deserves its own test.`,`And here's why I'm stubborn about this being a unit test and not an integration test: the moment you pull in a real database, you're testing the database, the network, the seed data, and — if you're lucky — your code. I want the opposite trade: zero external dependencies, millisecond feedback, and a failure that points at exactly one decision.`],quote:`A unit test doesn't test code — it tests one decision your code makes.`,table:{headers:[`Test Level`,`What It Touches`,`Feedback`,`Best For`],rows:[[`Unit (xUnit Fact)`,`One class, mocked seams`,`~milliseconds`,`Validation branches`],[`Integration`,`Real database, real repos`,`Seconds`,`SQL and mappings`],[`End-to-end`,`Whole deployed system`,`Minutes`,`Critical user journeys`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Naming and the arrange–act–assert rhythm`,body:[`Before I write a single assertion, I want the test to be readable at the point of failure. So the name follows a strict rhythm: method under test first — StartFollowingAsync — then Should, then the expectation, then When plus the trigger. StartFollowingAsync_Should_ReturnError_WhenFollowingSameUser. When that test goes red in CI, the name alone is the bug report.`,`Because the method is asynchronous, the test returns a Task — no .Result, no .Wait(), no deadlock roulette. And I structure every test as arrange, act, assert: build the user with User.Create passing an Email.Create, a Name.Create, and the hasPublicProfile flag; act by calling StartFollowingAsync — for the same-user case I literally pass the same user instance twice; then assert on the result's error.`,`Two small details people skip: I pass DateTime.UtcNow for the current time and default for the CancellationToken, because the signature demands them — and I'd rather be explicit at the call site than hide magic inside a helper I'll have to debug later.`],quote:`If the test name reads like a sentence, the failure report reads like a diagnosis.`,table:{headers:[`Naming Style`,`Example`,`Reads As`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Method_Should_When`,`StartFollowingAsync_Should_ReturnError_WhenFollowingSameUser`,`Diagnosis at a glance`,`Use it`],[`Scenario only`,`Test1 / SameUserTest`,`Requires opening the file`,`Avoid`],[`Given_When_Then prose`,`Given same user when follow then error`,`Nice in docs, noisy in runners`,`Optional`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,at:`2:01`}},{heading:`03 · Walking the boundary: why the repository must be a mock`,body:[`Now the part that actually makes this hard. The method calls an external service — the follower repository, via IsAlreadyFollowingAsync. In production that's a database round trip. In a unit test, that's forbidden territory. So I inject a mock for the follower repository and I take control of it.`,`With NSubstitute, the setup is almost embarrassingly readable: access the follower repository mock, specify the method — IsAlreadyFollowingAsync with the user ID, the followed user's ID, and the default cancellation token — then call Returns(true). That one line aims the if statement straight at the AlreadyFollowing branch. Want the other outcome? Returns(false) and you're on the success path.`,`And a warning from the trenches: an unconfigured mock doesn't throw — it quietly returns default. Forget the setup, NSubstitute hands you false, the branch never triggers, and your test can pass while asserting the wrong thing. Configure every external call you cross, or you're testing luck.`],quote:`You don't mock to fake the world — you mock to aim the code at the branch you want to hit.`,table:{headers:[`Test Double`,`Setup Cost`,`Fidelity`,`Unit-Test Fit`],rows:[[`NSubstitute mock`,`One .Returns() line`,`Only what you stub`,`Perfect`],[`Hand-written fake`,`A class per interface`,`Fixed in-memory behavior`,`Good for reuse`],[`Real database`,`Container + seed + cleanup`,`High, but slow and flaky`,`Save for integration tests`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,at:`5:42`}},{heading:`04 · Failure paths first: pinning every domain error`,body:[`Here's my ordering rule: failure paths first. They're the cheapest to pin down because they need the least ceremony — no successful repository round trip, no persisted follow record, just an input that trips a guard. Same user? Pass the instance twice. Non-public profile? Set hasPublicProfile to false when creating the followed user. Already following? Stub IsAlreadyFollowingAsync to return true.`,`Each branch returns a typed domain error — an error instance with a unique code living in the FollowerErrors class, like Followers.SameUserError. That's a gift for testing: my assertion isn't checking a string or a null, it's comparing against a documented, named constant. If someone changes the behavior, the test fails loudly at exactly the right spot.`,`Only after all three guards are pinned do I write the success case — stub the repository to return false and assert the result is a success. You'll clear the coverage number quickly, but the real win is that every error code in FollowerErrors now has a test that screams if its meaning drifts.`],quote:`Every error code in your domain errors class is a test waiting to be written.`,table:{headers:[`Branch`,`Trigger`,`Expected Error`,`Test`],rows:[[`Same user`,`Pass same user instance twice`,`Followers.SameUserError`,`Fact 1`],[`Non-public profile`,`hasPublicProfile = false`,`NonPublicProfile error`,`Fact 2`],[`Already following`,`Stub IsAlreadyFollowingAsync → true`,`AlreadyFollowing error`,`Fact 3`],[`Success`,`Stub IsAlreadyFollowingAsync → false`,`None — success result`,`Fact 4`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,at:`9:42`}},{heading:`05 · The cleanup pass: tests are code too`,body:[`First test is green — and that's my cue to clean up, not to move on. Look at the arrange section: I'm building the same email, the same name, the same service over and over. So I extract the FollowerService, the email, and the name into private readonly fields on the test class, instantiated once.`,`Now each new test only declares its delta: a different hasPublicProfile flag, a different stub return. The interesting part of every test — the thing that makes it unique — stops drowning in boilerplate. This is the same refactoring instinct you'd apply to production code, and tests deserve it more, because tests are read far more often than they're written.`,`One discipline to keep: extract shared setup, never shared assertions. The act and assert steps stay inline and visible in every test, because that's the part a reader must be able to verify at a glance.`],quote:`Green is the starting line, not the finish — refactor the test the moment it passes.`,table:{headers:[`Setup Style`,`Duplication`,`Per-Test Clarity`,`Maintenance`],rows:[[`Inline everything`,`High — copy-paste arrange`,`High, but noisy`,`Changes ripple everywhere`],[`Extracted fields`,`Low — one place`,`Delta stands out`,`Change once`],[`Giant base class`,`Lowest`,`Hidden magic`,`Hardest to debug`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,at:`13:22`}}],diagrams:[{id:`unit-test-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Unit test topology: the seam around FollowerService`,caption:`The test drives the real domain service; the repository is a stubbed seam, and the database is deliberately outside the boundary.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph TEST["Unit Test Project"]
        T["FollowerServiceTests<br/>xUnit Facts"]
        M["IFollowerRepository Mock<br/>NSubstitute .Returns()"]
    end
    subgraph DOMAIN["Domain Layer"]
        S["FollowerService<br/>StartFollowingAsync()"]
        E["FollowerErrors<br/>SameUser / NonPublicProfile / AlreadyFollowing"]
    end
    DB["Real Database<br/>not invited"]
    T -->|"arrange + act"| S
    T -->|"assert result.Error"| E
    S -->|"injected dependency seam"| M
    M -.->|"stubbed, never called for real"| DB`},{id:`start-following-branches`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`StartFollowingAsync decision path under test`,caption:`Each guard returns a typed domain error; the mock's .Returns(true) steers execution into the AlreadyFollowing branch.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    participant T as Test xUnit
    participant S as FollowerService
    participant R as IFollowerRepository Mock
    T->>S: StartFollowingAsync(user, followedUser, utcNow, ct)
    S->>S: same user id?
    alt same user
        S-->>T: FollowerErrors.SameUser
    else hasPublicProfile = false
        S-->>T: FollowerErrors.NonPublicProfile
    else candidate is valid
        S->>R: IsAlreadyFollowingAsync(userId, followedUserId, ct)
        R-->>S: true (stubbed via .Returns)
        S-->>T: FollowerErrors.AlreadyFollowing
    end`}],takeaways:[`One [Fact] per validation branch: three domain errors in StartFollowingAsync means at least three failure-path tests before you write the happy path.`,`Keep pure unit tests under ~50 ms each; if a test touches a database or the network, it's an integration test — rename it and quarantine it.`,`Name tests MethodName_Should_Expected_WhenCondition so a red CI row is a diagnosis without opening the file.`,`Explicitly stub every external call you cross with NSubstitute .Returns(...); an unconfigured mock silently returns default and can green-light the wrong behavior.`,`After the first green test, extract shared arrange steps into private readonly fields — it cuts setup duplication across the class by half or more while keeping act and assert inline.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — C# Unit Testing Best Practices for Great Code Coverage`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o_KVvUjwxIw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-kqYmyyyVNXw`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:8,title:`The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 15:30`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`AWS`,`.NET`,`LLM`],story:{headline:`AccessDeniedException at 2 a.m.: the demo that hinged on a checkbox`,paragraphs:[`It's 11 p.m. the night before a customer demo, and our .NET support-ticket summarizer is finally wired up. Semantic Kernel registered, prompt polished, Claude Sonnet selected in the Bedrock playground where it produced gorgeous summaries on the first try. I push the real integration, run it against a live ticket, and watch it die in under a second: AccessDeniedException.`,`Here's the thing nobody tells you at 11 p.m.: the Bedrock model catalog showing a model does not mean your account can call it. There are 55 base models in the catalog, and you have access to exactly the ones you've explicitly requested — scroll all the way down to Model access, pick your models, click through the end-user license agreement, and wait. I had skipped that step because the playground felt like proof. It wasn't.`,`By 2 a.m. access was granted and the demo was saved — and then I nearly re-killed it by ticking 'include model reasoning' because it sounded smart. Token count per request roughly tripled, latency climbed, and our cost projection for the pilot quietly doubled. The API was never the hard part. The gate, the EULA, and the token budget were.`],problem:`The code compiled, the prompt was validated in the playground, and the first real call still failed — because Bedrock model access is opt-in per account, and nobody had scrolled down to request it.`},tech:{stack:[`Amazon Bedrock`,`Semantic Kernel`,`.NET 8`,`AWS IAM`,`Claude Sonnet`,`Titan Embeddings`,`Bedrock Playground`],note:`Bedrock is the managed, IAM-governed gateway to a catalog of foundation models behind one API; Semantic Kernel is the .NET seam that keeps your application code decoupled from any single model.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just call one vendor and be done?`,body:[`The first question I always get is: why route through Bedrock at all when I could grab one vendor's SDK and ship? Honestly, for a weekend hack, you should. But the moment this becomes a product feature, you're signing up for that vendor's pricing, their rate limits, their breaking changes, and their roadmap. Bedrock flips that: one API, one IAM boundary, one AWS bill, and a catalog of 55-plus base models — Anthropic's Claude, Meta's Llama, Amazon's Nova and Titan, Cohere, Mistral, DeepSeek — sitting behind it.`,`For a .NET shop already living on AWS, the gravity is real. Your VPC endpoints, CloudWatch, and existing IAM roles just work, and your data stays in the region you picked. The trade-off is the access-gating workflow we'll walk next. I've made peace with that trade, because swapping models later becomes a config change instead of a migration project.`],quote:`The model is a config value, not an architecture decision.`,table:{headers:[`Path`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Direct vendor SDK`,`Day-one features, simplest setup`,`New SDK per model, hard lock-in`,`Single-model prototypes`],[`Amazon Bedrock`,`One API, many models, IAM + VPC native`,`Access gating, EULAs, console workflow`,`.NET shops already on AWS`],[`Self-hosted open model`,`Full control, no per-token cost`,`GPU ops burden is yours forever`,`Data that can't leave premises`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqYmyyyVNXw`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: model access is a gate, not a given`,body:[`Here's the part that burned us. You open the Bedrock console and the model catalog shows Claude Sonnet sitting right there, highlighted, beautiful. You assume it's yours. It is not. Scroll all the way down to Model access and you see the truth: 55 base models exist, and your account can invoke exactly the ones you've explicitly requested.`,`The flow is: select the model, request access, click through any end-user license agreement, and wait. Most Amazon and Anthropic models grant access in minutes; some third-party models go to manual review. Either way, this is a console-and-IAM workflow, not a code workflow — which means it belongs in your onboarding runbook and your infrastructure-as-code, not in somebody's memory.`,`The 2 a.m. version of me learned this the hard way so the 9 a.m. version of you gets the shortcut: request access for every model you might touch, in every region you deploy to, before the sprint starts.`],quote:`The hardest API call in Bedrock is the one you make in the console, not in C#.`,table:{headers:[`Access state`,`What it means`,`Time to first call`],rows:[[`Not requested`,`Visible in catalog, every invoke denied`,`Never`],[`Instant EULA grant`,`Accept license, access lands in minutes`,`~5 minutes`],[`Manual review`,`AWS approves per use case`,`Hours to days`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqYmyyyVNXw`,at:`1:18`}},{heading:`03 · The token bill nobody reads until the invoice`,body:[`Once you're in the playground, the temptation is to tick every box. The dangerous one is 'include model reasoning.' It's genuinely useful — the model shows its work and multi-step quality goes up — but every reasoning token is billed and latency climbs with it. On our summarizer, flipping that toggle roughly tripled tokens per request.`,`The same math applies to system prompts: they ride along on every single call, so a 500-token persona is 500 tokens times your call volume, forever. Set max tokens deliberately, pick temperature per use case — near zero for extraction, higher for drafting — and log the usage metrics Bedrock returns on every response from day one. You cannot manage a cost curve you're not measuring.`],quote:`Reasoning tokens are rent with interest — pay them only when the problem earns it.`,table:{headers:[`Setting`,`Reasoning off`,`Reasoning on`],rows:[[`Tokens per request`,`Baseline`,`2-4x baseline`],[`Latency`,`Lower`,`Noticeably higher`],[`Cost predictability`,`Easy to budget`,`Budget with headroom`],[`Answer quality`,`Fine for summaries`,`Better for multi-step logic`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqYmyyyVNXw`,at:`2:23`}},{heading:`04 · Wiring it into .NET: Semantic Kernel as the seam`,body:[`Now the fun part, the C#. Semantic Kernel is the seam: add the Bedrock connector package, register it with your model ID from appsettings, and your application code talks to an abstraction, not to Claude directly. The day you want Haiku for cheap drafts and Sonnet for final output, you're changing configuration, not rewriting handlers.`,`My workflow: prototype the prompt in the Bedrock playground until the output is actually good, then port it verbatim into a Semantic Kernel function with the system prompt in config. And if RAG is on your roadmap, Titan embeddings through the same gateway means your vector pipeline speaks the same IAM dialect as your chat pipeline — one boundary, one audit trail.`],quote:`Prototype in the playground, productionize in C# — never the other way around.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Playground first`,`Iterate prompts in seconds`,`Manual, not reproducible`,`Prompt discovery`],[`Code first`,`Reproducible, testable`,`Slow prompt iteration`,`Once prompt is stable`],[`Model ID in config`,`Swap models freely`,`Needs team discipline`,`Every real deployment`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqYmyyyVNXw`,at:`3:20`}}],diagrams:[{id:`bedrock-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Bedrock topology for a .NET app`,caption:`One IAM-governed gateway in front of the whole model catalog — the app never talks to a vendor directly.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
  subgraph APP["ASP.NET Core App"]
    SVC["Ticket Summarizer"]
    SK["Semantic Kernel"]
  end
  subgraph AWS["AWS Account"]
    IAM["IAM Role"]
    GATE["Model Access Gate"]
    BR["Amazon Bedrock Runtime"]
    subgraph CAT["Model Catalog - 55+ base models"]
      CL["Claude Sonnet"]
      TIT["Titan Embeddings"]
      LL["Llama"]
      NV["Nova"]
    end
  end
  SVC --> SK
  SK -- "InvokeModel / Converse" --> BR
  IAM -. "signs every request" .-> SK
  GATE -- "opt-in per model + EULA" --> BR
  BR --> CAT`},{id:`summarizer-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Anatomy of a summarizer call`,caption:`The happy path, plus the AccessDeniedException branch that killed our demo at 11 p.m.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant App as .NET Service
  participant SK as Semantic Kernel
  participant BR as Bedrock Runtime
  participant M as Claude Sonnet
  App->>SK: Summarize(ticket)
  SK->>SK: Build prompt + system prompt
  SK->>BR: Converse(modelId, messages)
  BR->>BR: Check IAM + model access
  alt access granted
    BR->>M: Invoke with maxTokens, temperature
    M-->>BR: Completion + token usage
    BR-->>SK: Response + usage metrics
    SK-->>App: Summary
  else access not requested
    BR-->>SK: AccessDeniedException
    SK-->>App: Fail fast - link to Model Access console
  end`}],takeaways:[`Request model access before writing a line of code — budget 15 minutes per model for EULAs, and repeat the request in every region you deploy to.`,`Treat the model ID as configuration: swapping Claude Haiku for Sonnet should be a one-line appsettings change, never a refactor.`,`Cap reasoning per feature — leaving 'include model reasoning' on can inflate token consumption 2-4x and your latency with it.`,`Validate every prompt in the Bedrock playground first; five minutes there saves an hour of deploy-debug cycles in C#.`,`Log input and output token counts from day one — a 4K-token summarizer at 50K calls a month is a finance conversation, not an engineering one.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Fastest Way to Add AI to Your .NET App: Amazon Bedrock`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kqYmyyyVNXw`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`ai-llm-concepts`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-Cpej__Ns1sA`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:9,title:`I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:18`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`AWS`,`.NET`,`LLM`],story:{headline:`A few dozen green tests at 1 AM — and I didn't trust a single one of them`,paragraphs:[`It's late on a weeknight and Run Tracker — my .NET 9 clean-architecture side project — needs test coverage before I can feel good about the workout endpoints. I have 31 passing tests across four test projects, and the honest math says hand-writing coverage for the whole Domain project will eat the rest of my evening. So I do the thing we all do now: I open Cursor, reference my rules file, point it at the Domain project, and hit enter on agent mode.`,`Three minutes later it's done. A pile of new xUnit tests — Arrange-Act-Assert, my naming convention, everything green on the very first run. And that's exactly when the hair on my neck goes up: green on the first run means the tests never had a chance to fail. I open one written against the add-exercises command handler, and it asserts precisely what the handler currently does — bug or not. The agent didn't test my intent. It interviewed my implementation and wrote down what it heard.`,`That night taught me the actual shape of this job. The AI is phenomenal at the typing and mediocre at the judgment. The fix wasn't a cleverer prompt — it was the boring scaffolding I almost skipped: a green baseline to imitate, a rules file that encodes my taste, tightly scoped prompts, and a review pass where I break production code on purpose and watch which tests scream.`],problem:`AI-generated tests go green because they agree with the code as written, not with the behavior you meant. Without a baseline, rules, and an adversarial review pass, you aren't buying coverage — you're buying a mirror.`},tech:{stack:[`Cursor`,`.NET 9`,`xUnit`,`Clean Architecture`,`In-Memory API`,`Cursor Rules`],note:`Cursor's agent mode drives test generation over a .NET 9 clean-architecture solution; xUnit with Arrange-Act-Assert is the enforced convention, architecture tests pin layer dependencies, and functional tests hit an in-memory API host through an HTTP client.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: just point the agent at the repo, right?`,body:[`So the lazy version of this is obvious: open the AI, say 'write tests for my application,' and walk away. I tried that mental model first because it's the one every demo sells you. What you get back is technically tests — a random framework mix, invented helpers, happy paths only, and a style that matches nothing else in your repo. The model isn't dumb; it just has no contract.`,`The realization for me was that test writing is a convention-heavy activity. Arrange-Act-Assert, naming, what counts as 'interesting behavior,' which framework — those decisions are most of the value, and the model will happily make them for you, badly, unless you take them away. So the whole technique flips: don't ask the AI what tests to write. Tell it what a good test looks like, and where to hunt.`],quote:`An agent without conventions doesn't write your tests — it writes its tests in your repo.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Point agent at repo, 'test it'`,`Zero setup, instant output`,`Random style, shallow coverage, framework soup`,`Never`],[`Baseline + rules + scoped prompt`,`Matches conventions, reviewable diffs`,`Requires upfront scaffolding`,`Default choice`],[`Hand-write every test`,`Full judgment, perfect intent`,`Hours per feature, gaps appear at 5 PM`,`Tricky domain logic only`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · The baseline: 31 green tests before the agent wakes up`,body:[`Here's the part people skip, and it's the whole game: before I let Cursor touch anything, I already had four test projects green — domain unit tests for the User entity, unit tests for the application use cases, architecture tests that validate the dependencies between my layers, and functional tests that spin up an in-memory API instance and poke it with an HTTP client. Thirty-one tests, all passing. The number matters less than what it represents: a definition of done for style.`,`Those existing tests are the template. They say: we use xUnit, we use Arrange-Act-Assert, we name tests this way, and functional tests return either a deserialized object or the raw HTTP response so we can assert status codes. When I prompt the agent, I explicitly say 'use the user tests as the baseline of what a good test looks like.' The agent imitates far better than it invents — so give it something to imitate.`,`And the architecture tests are a quiet superpower here. They're the tripwire that stops an eager agent from 'solving' a test problem by referencing the domain from the API project or smuggling a dependency across a layer. The agent can't violate a rule it gets yelled at for by the build.`],quote:`Your existing tests aren't a starting point — they're the style guide the agent will imitate.`,table:{headers:[`Test project`,`What it pins down`,`What it catches`],rows:[[`Domain unit tests`,`Entity + domain service behavior, AAA, naming`,`Broken invariants, lost edge cases`],[`Application unit tests`,`Use case and command handler contracts`,`Handler logic drift`],[`Architecture tests`,`Allowed dependencies between layers`,`Agents (and humans) cheating across layers`],[`Functional tests`,`HTTP contract via in-memory API + client`,`Status code and response-shape regressions`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,at:`1:01`}},{heading:`03 · Cursor rules: a code review before the code exists`,body:[`The rules file is where I put everything the agent should know before it types. Mine has four sections: context about the solution, planning rules, formatting rules, and additional rules specific to how I want my .NET code written. Nothing clever — it's the same feedback I'd leave on a pull request, written down once so I never have to leave it again.`,`The trick is referencing it explicitly. Every prompt attaches the cursor rules file and the golden test file together. That pairing — 'here are the laws, here is one worked example' — is what turns the output from 'a test' into 'one of my tests.' And keep it tight: a page of sharp constraints beats a ten-page essay the model will skim.`],quote:`A rules file is a code review you do before the code exists.`,table:{headers:[`Rules section`,`What it says`,`Failure it prevents`],rows:[[`Context`,`What the solution is, where projects live`,`Agent guessing at structure`],[`Planning rules`,`List the cases before writing code`,`Stream-of-consciousness tests`],[`Formatting rules`,`AAA, naming convention, one behavior per test`,`Style drift across the suite`],[`.NET-specific rules`,`xUnit, idiomatic C#, no surprise packages`,`Framework soup`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,at:`2:00`}},{heading:`04 · Agent mode: the loop, and where it wobbles`,body:[`Agent mode is the part that feels like magic: it iterates over the codebase until it satisfies the requirements. My prompt is deliberately scoped — 'check the Domain project, scan the entities and domain services, write unit tests covering all the interesting behavior, and use the user test project as your baseline.' Scoped beats sweeping. One project, one folder reference, one definition of interesting.`,`The loop is real: it reads, writes, builds, runs, and fixes itself when a test doesn't compile. That's where it earns its keep — bulk coverage, edge cases you'd skip at 5 PM, the tedious 80 percent. Where it wobbles is judgment: it will sometimes assert what the code does instead of what it should do, write a tautology, or over-mock until the test proves nothing. Green is not the goal. Failable is the goal.`],quote:`Agent mode is a junior dev with infinite patience and no opinions — your rules file is the opinions.`,table:{headers:[`Task`,`Agent's grade`,`Your move`],rows:[[`Bulk unit tests for entities + handlers`,`Excellent`,`Skim for style drift`],[`Edge cases on domain invariants`,`Good when nudged`,`Name the cases you care about`],[`Asserts matching intent, not implementation`,`Risky`,`Read every assert`],[`Layer dependency rules`,`Follows if enforced`,`Keep architecture tests in the loop`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,at:`3:21`}},{heading:`05 · The review is the job now`,body:[`After the agent finishes, my workflow is not 'merge.' It's a five-minute adversarial pass: I read the asserts, not the test names, and then I do the thing I wish someone had taught me years ago — I break a line in the command handler on purpose and rerun the suite. If a new test doesn't go red, it was decoration. Names lie; a killed mutant doesn't.`,`And honestly, that reframing is the takeaway. The AI moved me from typing tests to reviewing intent, and the throughput gain is real — but only because the scaffolding makes the output reviewable: green baseline, tight rules, scoped prompts, architecture tests as guardrails. Skip the scaffolding and you get the 1 AM mirror: a pile of green tests that agree with your bugs.`],quote:`A test that can't fail is a comment with delusions of grandeur.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,at:`4:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Run Tracker: solution topology and where each test project bites`,caption:`Clean architecture keeps dependencies pointing inward; the four test projects each pin a different layer — and the architecture tests are the tripwire that keeps the agent from cheating across boundaries.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph SRC["RunTracker solution - .NET 9, Clean Architecture"]
        API["Api project<br/>user + workout endpoints"]
        APP["Application project<br/>use cases + command handlers<br/>e.g. add exercises"]
        DOM["Domain project<br/>entities + domain services"]
        API --> APP
        APP --> DOM
    end
    subgraph TST["Four test projects - 31 green tests"]
        DU["Domain unit tests<br/>xUnit, AAA"]
        AU["Application unit tests"]
        AT["Architecture tests<br/>layer dependency rules"]
        FT["Functional tests<br/>in-memory API + HTTP client"]
    end
    DU --> DOM
    AU --> APP
    AT -.-> API
    AT -.-> APP
    AT -.-> DOM
    FT --> API`},{id:`agent-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The agent loop: scoped prompt in, reviewable tests out`,caption:`Agent mode iterates read, write, run, fix until the requirements are met — the human's job starts the moment the loop ends.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Dev as Developer
    participant Agent as Cursor agent mode
    participant Repo as RunTracker repo
    participant Test as dotnet test

    Dev->>Agent: Reference cursor rules file + UserTests baseline
    Dev->>Agent: Prompt: cover Domain project behavior
    Agent->>Repo: Scan entities and domain services
    Agent->>Repo: Read baseline tests for style
    loop until requirements satisfied
        Agent->>Repo: Write xUnit tests (AAA, naming convention)
        Agent->>Test: Build and run test project
        Test-->>Agent: Pass / fail results
        Agent->>Repo: Fix compile or assertion failures
    end
    Agent-->>Dev: New tests, all green
    Dev->>Test: Run full suite (31+ tests)
    Dev->>Dev: Break a handler line - which tests go red?`}],takeaways:[`Green baseline first: get all four test projects passing (31 in my case) before the agent writes a line — you can't review generation without a reference point.`,`Keep the rules file to roughly one page across four sections — context, planning, formatting, .NET-specific — sharp constraints beat essays the model will skim.`,`Attach exactly two artifacts to every prompt: the rules file and one golden test file; imitation is the agent's strongest mode.`,`Scope prompts to one project at a time — 'scan the entities and domain services in Domain' outperforms 'test the app' by an order of magnitude.`,`Review by mutation, not by reading names: break one line of a handler and rerun — if no new test goes red, delete that test.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I used AI to write tests for my .NET application... Here's what happened`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cpej__Ns1sA`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`ai-coding-workflow`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-wgNZWnua-90`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:10,title:`I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 18:13`,minutes:18,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`C#`,`LLM`],story:{headline:`98.57% Accurate and Confidently Wrong: The Night My Hand-Built C# Neural Network Called My Own Initial a '4'`,paragraphs:[`There's a notebook on my shelf I haven't opened in seven years. First page: a hand-drawn diagram of a neural network that recognizes digits — 28×28 pixels in, ten probabilities out. Second page: calculus. I built that thing in pure C#, no TensorFlow, no ML.NET, just matrices, derivatives, and stubbornness. And at some point around 1 AM back then, I was staring at a training loop that refused to learn.`,`The loss was pinned at 2.3026. Not drifting, not spiking — pinned. I should have recognized that number sooner, because 2.3026 is exactly ln(10): the loss of a network that shrugs and outputs a uniform guess across ten digits. Accuracy: ~10%, coin-flip territory for a ten-sided coin. The bug wasn't in the math. It was in the wiring — I'd fed a layer producing 64 outputs into a layer expecting 128 inputs, and instead of throwing an exception, my code happily trained garbage.`,`Fast-forward to the demo that made it all worth it, and then immediately humbled me. My best model hits 98.57% on the test set. I draw a clean digit, it nails it. Then, feeling clever, I draw the first letter of my name. The network doesn't hesitate for a millisecond: 'That's a 4,' it says — with the confidence of a system that has never once been asked to say 'I don't know.' That moment is what this lesson is really about.`],problem:`How do you build, train, and honestly evaluate a neural network in plain C# — and where does the from-scratch approach break, both in the math and in your assumptions about what 'confident' means?`},tech:{stack:[`C#`,`.NET Framework`,`WinForms`,`MNIST Digits`,`Backpropagation`,`Gradient Descent`,`Dropout`,`Learning Pipeline`],note:`A from-scratch stack: plain C# on .NET Framework with a WinForms shell, hand-rolled backpropagation over MNIST digits, and every training knob exposed through one configurable learning pipeline.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why not just use a library?`,body:[`Fair question, and I ask it every time I'm three hours into debugging matrix multiplication. ML.NET, an ONNX runtime, a Python service behind an HTTP call — any of them gets you a digit classifier in an afternoon. But you'd learn exactly one thing: how to call a library. The moment your model misbehaves in production, a black box gives you nothing to grip.`,`From scratch, every failure is yours and every fix is a lesson. I re-learned why weight initialization matters (all zeros means every neuron computes the same thing and nothing ever differentiates), why the derivative is the steering wheel, and why shape mismatches fail silently. And honestly, C# is plenty fast at MNIST scale — 784 floats per image is nothing. The trade-off is real though: no GPU acceleration story, no pretrained anything, and you own every line of the math.`],quote:`A library gets you a model by Friday. From scratch gets you the judgment you'll need the Monday it breaks.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`From scratch in C#`,`Total understanding; zero dependencies; full control of the training loop`,`Slow iteration; you own every bug; no GPU or pretrained models`,`Learning + small-scale inference`],[`ML library (ML.NET / ONNX)`,`Fast to ship; battle-tested math; pretrained options`,`Black-box debugging; runtime and version drag`,`Production inference at scale`],[`Python + PyTorch behind an API`,`Huge ecosystem; GPU training`,`Extra service, latency, ops surface`,`Heavy training workloads`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wgNZWnua-90`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: every layer has a shape contract`,body:[`Here's the architecture from page one of the notebook. A 28×28 grayscale image flattens into a 784-element vector — that's the input layer. Then hidden layers, my choice how many and how wide, and finally an output layer with exactly 10 neurons, one per digit, pushed through softmax so the outputs read as probabilities that sum to one.`,`The one rule I'd tattoo on every ML engineer's wrist: the number of outputs from layer N must equal the number of inputs to layer N+1. It sounds trivial until it's 1 AM and your loss is pinned at ln(10) because you wired a 64-output layer into a 128-input layer and nothing crashed. In hand-rolled code, shape mismatches don't throw exceptions — they just quietly teach your network nothing.`,`So now I write the shape contract down before I code a single layer: 784 in, hidden layers in between, 10 out, softmax at the end. It's the same discipline as an API contract between services — except the compiler won't catch you when you break it.`],quote:`Shape mismatches don't crash — they just quietly train garbage.`,table:{headers:[`Layer`,`Inputs`,`Outputs`,`Activation`],rows:[[`Input`,`784 (28×28 flattened)`,`784`,`—`],[`Hidden 1`,`784`,`e.g. 128`,`ReLU`],[`Hidden 2`,`128`,`e.g. 64`,`ReLU`],[`Output`,`64`,`10`,`Softmax`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wgNZWnua-90`,at:`6:40`}},{heading:`03 · The training loop: calculus as a steering wheel`,body:[`Training is gradient descent, and the mental model is almost embarrassingly simple: your network's error is a landscape, and you're standing on a slope. The first derivative at your current position tells you which way is uphill. So you step the other way. Wash, rinse, repeat, and the error walks toward zero.`,`But the knobs are where the engineering lives. Learning rate too big and you leap clean over the valley; too small and you'll retire before the model converges. Gradients can explode, so you clip them. The network will happily memorize your training set, so you add regularization and dropout to keep it honest. And you'd better initialize weights randomly, or every neuron learns the same thing and the network never differentiates at all.`,`The move I'm proudest of is architectural, not mathematical: I wrapped all of it — learning rate, gradient clipping, regularization, dropout, weight initializer, and the layer stack — behind one 'learning pipeline' abstraction. A training experiment becomes a config change, not a code change. That's the same instinct as feature flags or a strategy pattern in any backend: isolate the thing you're going to tweak fifty times.`],quote:`Training is walking downhill in the dark, one derivative at a time.`,table:{headers:[`Knob`,`What it buys you`,`When it bites`],rows:[[`Learning rate`,`Speed of convergence`,`Too high: diverges; too low: crawls`],[`Gradient clipping`,`Stability against exploding gradients`,`Masks a badly scaled problem if overused`],[`Dropout`,`Fights overfitting`,`Slows training; wrong rate hurts accuracy`],[`Regularization (L2)`,`Simpler, generalizing weights`,`Too strong: underfits`],[`Weight initializer`,`Breaks symmetry between neurons`,`All-zeros: the network never learns`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wgNZWnua-90`,at:`10:42`}},{heading:`04 · Proving it: benchmark before you brag`,body:[`The WinForms presentation app — yes, .NET Framework, this thing is a fossil and I love it — has a Run Benchmark button. It feeds the test set through the network 100 images at a time, takes the argmax of each probability vector, and compares it to the true label. My best model: 98.57%.`,`But here's the habit that actually made me better: watching the misses. A '2' that the network swears is an '8' teaches you more than the other 98 correct answers combined. Averages hide your failure modes; the confusion cases are where your next improvement lives.`,`I also built a sliding-window detector that crawls the screen looking for digit-shaped pixel clusters to classify. It works... generously. Turns out 'where is the digit' is a whole separate problem from 'what digit is this' — segmentation versus classification — and my from-scratch network only solves the second one.`],quote:`Your benchmark number is an average. Your users live in the misses.`,table:{headers:[`Evaluation method`,`What it tells you`,`Blind spot`],rows:[[`Test-set benchmark (batches of 100)`,`Aggregate accuracy: 98.57%`,`Hides which digits fail and why`],[`Hand-drawn input`,`Real-world robustness feel`,`Still in-domain — it's still a digit`],[`Sliding-window scan`,`Crude localization attempt`,`Segmentation is unsolved here: noisy and fragile`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wgNZWnua-90`,at:`14:41`}},{heading:`05 · The supervised trap: confidence is not correctness`,body:[`Back to the letter M. Softmax forces the ten outputs to sum to one, so the network must distribute its confidence somewhere — it has no vocabulary for 'none of the above.' Show it something outside its world and it doesn't hesitate; it picks the nearest-looking digit and commits.`,`That's the core limitation of supervised learning: you get a model that can only classify what you trained it on, and it will be confidently wrong about everything else. In a production system that's not a cute demo quirk — that's a support-ticket generator. The fix lives outside the network: an out-of-distribution check, a confidence threshold, a reject path that says 'I don't know' before the wrong answer ships.`],quote:`A classifier never says 'I don't know' — you have to build that part yourself.`}],diagrams:[{id:`network-architecture`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Digit recognizer: forward pass and training loop`,caption:`28×28 pixels become 784 floats, flow through the layer stack into a 10-way softmax, and backpropagation pushes the error signal back into every weight.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    IMG["28x28 grayscale image"] -->|"flatten: 784 floats"| IN["Input layer (784)"]
    IN --> H1["Hidden layer (128, ReLU)"]
    H1 --> H2["Hidden layer (64, ReLU)"]
    H2 --> OUT["Output layer (10, softmax)"]
    OUT -->|"probabilities 0-9"| PRED["Prediction: argmax"]
    PRED -->|"compare to true label"| LOSS["Loss: cross-entropy"]
    LOSS -->|"backprop"| BP["Gradients per weight"]
    BP -->|"gradient descent step"| W["All weights updated"]
    W -.->|"next batch of 100 images"| IN`},{id:`benchmark-sequence`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Benchmark run — and the humbling demo`,caption:`The WinForms app streams the test set in batches of 100, scores accuracy against true labels, then meets its match: an out-of-distribution sketch that gets a confident wrong answer.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor U as Presenter
    participant App as WinForms app (.NET Framework)
    participant Net as Trained network (98.57%)
    participant DS as MNIST test set

    U->>App: Click Run Benchmark
    App->>DS: Load test images and labels
    loop every batch of 100 images
        App->>Net: Forward pass (100 x 784)
        Net-->>App: Probability vectors (100 x 10)
        App->>App: argmax vs true label
    end
    App-->>U: Accuracy report + misclassified samples (e.g. 8 vs 2)
    U->>App: Draw the letter M on canvas
    App->>Net: Classify sketch
    Net-->>U: Says 4 — confidently wrong`}],takeaways:[`If a 10-class loss is pinned at ln(10) ≈ 2.30 with ~10% accuracy, your network is guessing — audit layer shape alignment (outputs of layer N = inputs of layer N+1) before touching any hyperparameter.`,`Write the shape contract before the code: 28×28 → 784 inputs → hidden layers of your choosing → 10 softmax outputs; one misaligned wire trains garbage silently.`,`Benchmark in batches of ~100 images and read the misses: at 98.57% accuracy, roughly 1 in every 70 test digits is wrong, and those failures define your real quality ceiling.`,`Never ship softmax confidence as truth: an out-of-distribution scribble still gets a confident label, so put an explicit in-domain gate or reject threshold in front of the model.`,`Isolate every training knob — learning rate, gradient clipping, dropout, regularization, weight initializer — behind one pipeline config, so a hyperparameter A/B costs a restart, not a refactor.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I Built a Neural Network in C# From Scratch. Here’s What I Learned…`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wgNZWnua-90`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`ai-llm-concepts`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-uewVaWtI9fs`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:11,title:`Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:07`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`C#`],story:{headline:`Two million price points and a 3 AM page`,paragraphs:[`It's 09:30 during a latency regression audit and the pricing service's p99 has climbed from 40 ms to 4.2 seconds. Every market lookup fans out into a 'get the price at or below X' call, and that call is walking a sorted list of two million price points front to back — on every single request. The dashboards are red, the retry storm is making everything worse, and someone in the incident channel asks the question that ends every one of these nights: 'wait... why is this a loop?'`,`The profiler takes five minutes to confirm what a code review should have caught in 2019: one method is eating 90% of the CPU, and it's a for loop comparing element after element of an array that has been sorted since the day it was created — sorted, ironically, so a UI could display prices in order. The team had been sitting on O(log n) for years and paying O(n) every tick. The fix is fifteen lines: low, high, mid, compare, halve. p99 lands at 11 ms and the page clears before sunrise.`,`The kicker: nobody on that call had ever written a binary search 'in anger'. Everyone knew the name. Almost nobody could reproduce the boundary conditions from memory without an off-by-one — which is exactly why this lesson exists.`],problem:`Every lookup linearly scanned up to 2,000,000 sorted elements; binary search answers the same query in at most 21 comparisons — but only if the sorted invariant, the midpoint math, and the boundary updates are exactly right.`},tech:{stack:[`C#`,`.NET`,`Array.BinarySearch`,`Sorted Arrays`,`Big-O Analysis`],note:`A sorted in-memory array, the classic low/high/mid loop, and the BCL's built-in binary search — the entire toolkit for turning O(n) lookups into O(log n).`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why the linear scan dies at 2 AM`,body:[`Let me give you the analogy that makes this stick. Take a book — say, Clean Architecture with .NET — and try to find a chapter you know starts around page 219. Nobody flips one page at a time. You open the book in the middle, land on page 173, and instantly know everything to the left is dead to you. Split again: page 241. Now you know it's between 173 and 241. Three flips and you're within a few pages of the answer. That's the entire trick — exploit order to throw away half the search space every step.`,`Now translate that to the incident. The hot path was doing the 'flip one page at a time' thing against two million sorted price points. That's O(n): double the data, double the latency, every single Black Friday. And here's the painful part — the array was already sorted. The information needed to halve the search space was sitting right there, and the code never used it.`,`Could we have just thrown more hardware at it? No — and this is the point I want to land early. O(n) versus O(log n) is not a constant-factor win you can buy your way out of. At a million elements it's roughly 500,000 comparisons versus 20. Traffic grows, data grows, and a linear curve grows right along with both. No instance size fixes that.`],quote:`Every linear scan is a promise to read the whole book when you only need one chapter.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Linear scan`,`Dead simple, cache-friendly, works unsorted`,`O(n) — dies as data grows`,`Tiny arrays, one-off lookups`],[`Binary search`,`O(log n), range-aware, zero extra memory`,`Needs sorted data, fiddly boundaries`,`Sorted arrays, hot lookup paths`],[`Hash set`,`O(1) average membership`,`No ranges, no order, no nearest-match`,`Pure 'is it there?' checks`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uewVaWtI9fs`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: low, high, and one famous overflow`,body:[`The whole algorithm is three variables. Set low = 0 and high = n - 1, then loop while low <= high. Compute mid, compare arr[mid] to the target: equal means return mid; target smaller means high = mid - 1; target larger means low = mid + 1. If the loop exits, the element isn't there — return -1, the conventional 'no such index'. That's it. That's the algorithm.`,`But the midpoint line matters more than people think. The naive (low + high) / 2 overflows a 32-bit int once low + high passes about 2.1 billion — and this exact bug shipped inside the JDK's own binary search for roughly nine years before Josh Bloch wrote it up in 2006. C# has the same trap with unchecked int math. The safe form is mid = low + (high - low) / 2, which mathematically cannot overflow because you're always adding a non-negative half-gap.`,`The other killer is off-by-one on the boundaries. You must loop on low <= high (not low < high, or you miss the case where the range narrows to a single element), and you must step to mid - 1 and mid + 1 (not mid, or you spin forever on a one-element range). Knuth likes to remind us that binary search was first published in 1946 and the first version correct on all arrays didn't appear until 1962. Ten lines of code, sixteen years of bugs.`],quote:`Binary search is ten lines of code and a hundred ways to write them wrong.`,table:{headers:[`Classic bug`,`Symptom`,`Fix`],rows:[[`(low + high) / 2`,`Negative index via int overflow on huge arrays`,`low + (high - low) / 2`],[`Loop on low < high`,`Misses the target when low == high`,`Loop on low <= high`],[`high = mid`,`Infinite loop on a one-element range`,`high = mid - 1`],[`Unsorted input`,`Silently wrong answers, no exception thrown`,`Assert sorted, or sort first`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uewVaWtI9fs`,at:`1:38`}},{heading:`03 · The sorted prerequisite — and 'why not just a HashSet?'`,body:[`Here's the one non-negotiable: the array must be sorted in ascending order. Binary search has no magic — it's purely exploiting order to discard half the world each step. The economics are great, though: sorting once costs O(n log n), and every lookup after that is O(log n). Rough rule: the sort pays for itself after about log n lookups, which on any real workload is almost immediately.`,`Now the question I get in every Q&A: 'why not just use a HashSet?' Fair. O(1) membership is fantastic — if all you need is 'is this price in the set?'. But a hash set can't tell you the cheapest price at or below X, the first entry greater than or equal to Y, or iterate in order. A sorted array answers all of those, because binary search degrades gracefully into lower-bound and upper-bound semantics. Even the failure is useful: in .NET, Array.BinarySearch returns the bitwise complement (~) of the index of the next-larger element — a free insertion-point finder, not just a -1.`,`In the incident, we didn't need new infrastructure at all. The array was already sorted for display purposes, so we swapped the scan for Array.BinarySearch and kept a hand-rolled loop only where we needed a lower-bound variant. Fifteen lines, zero schema changes, order of magnitude off the latency.`],quote:`A hash set answers 'is it there?' — a sorted array answers 'where does it belong?'`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uewVaWtI9fs`,at:`6:20`}},{heading:`04 · The log2 payoff — and when NOT to binary search`,body:[`Let's put numbers on the payoff, because the curve is the whole business case. log2(1,000) is about 10. log2(1,000,000) is about 20. log2(1,000,000,000) is about 30. You can add a billion elements and pay ten extra comparisons. That's why the p99 fell off a cliff that night: two million elements went from roughly a million average comparisons to 21, worst case.`,`But don't binary-search everything — that's the contrarian slide. Below roughly 64 elements, linear scan frequently wins the benchmark, because cache lines and branch prediction beat asymptotics at small n. And if your data is unsorted and you'll only look up once or twice, sorting first at O(n log n) costs more than the linear scan you were trying to avoid. Binary search is a bet that lookups will be repeated.`,`In C#, my honest guidance: reach for Array.BinarySearch or MemoryExtensions.BinarySearch on Span<T> in production code — the BCL version is correct and tested. But write the low/high/mid loop by hand at least once, ideally on a whiteboard, so the boundary conditions live in your fingers instead of in a blog post you half-remember at 3 AM.`],quote:`O(log n) is a superpower — until cache lines and branch predictors out-vote it at small n.`,table:{headers:[`Scenario`,`Pick`,`Why`],rows:[[`Sorted array, hot lookups`,`Binary search`,`~20 probes per million items`],[`Under ~64 elements`,`Linear scan`,`Cache locality beats asymptotics`],[`Membership only, no order needed`,`HashSet`,`O(1) average, no sort required`],[`One lookup, unsorted data`,`Linear scan`,`Sorting costs more than the scan`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uewVaWtI9fs`,at:`10:35`}}],diagrams:[{id:`binary-search-loop`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The binary search control loop`,caption:`The full decision loop: halve the range, compare at mid, and either return the index or fall out to -1.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    S["Start: low = 0, high = n - 1"] --> C{"low <= high ?"}
    C -- "no" --> NF["Return -1 — not found"]
    C -- "yes" --> M["mid = low + (high - low) / 2"]
    M --> E{"arr[mid] == target ?"}
    E -- "yes" --> F["Return mid"]
    E -- "target smaller" --> L["high = mid - 1"]
    E -- "target larger" --> R["low = mid + 1"]
    L --> C
    R --> C`},{id:`linear-vs-binary-probes`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Probe count on [1, 3, 7, 11, 17, 29, 31], target 17`,caption:`Same array, same target: the linear scan touches 5 elements; binary search touches 3 and is done.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph LIN ["Linear scan — 5 probes"]
        direction LR
        A1["idx 0 · 1"] --> A2["idx 1 · 3"] --> A3["idx 2 · 7"] --> A4["idx 3 · 11"] --> A5["idx 4 · 17 ✓"]
    end
    subgraph BIN ["Binary search — 3 probes"]
        direction LR
        B1["low 0 · high 6 · mid 3 → 11"] -->|"17 > 11, go right"| B2["low 4 · high 6 · mid 5 → 29"] -->|"17 < 29, go left"| B3["low 4 · high 4 · mid 4 → 17 ✓"]
    end`}],takeaways:[`A million sorted items take ~20 binary-search probes versus ~500,000 average linear comparisons — log2(n) is the entire business case.`,`Sort once at O(n log n); the sort pays for itself after roughly log n lookups, so any repeatedly-queried collection should be sorted.`,`Always compute mid = low + (high - low) / 2 — the naive (low + high) / 2 overflows int past ~2.1 billion elements (the JDK shipped this bug for ~9 years).`,`Loop while low <= high and step with mid - 1 / mid + 1 — any looser boundary hands you an infinite loop or a silently missed element.`,`Below ~64 elements, linear scan often benchmarks faster thanks to cache locality — measure before you 'optimize', and prefer Array.BinarySearch (whose negative result encodes the insertion point via ~index) over a hand-rolled loop in production.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Binary Search Algorithm in C#`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uewVaWtI9fs`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-timsort`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-ASa8wXMXwrQ`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:12,title:`The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 16:23`,minutes:16,difficulty:3,tags:[`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Green Build That Lied: 214 Passing Tests, Zero Working Signups`,paragraphs:[`It was 15:40 as connection pools saturated on a Monday when the on-call phone lit up: every user signup since the Friday deploy was returning a 500. The Friday CI run had been a sea of green — 214 tests, all passing. The cruel part came in the postmortem: the bug lived in a Postgres-specific failure path, a unique-constraint violation surfacing as an unmapped DbUpdateException, and our integration tests literally could not see it because they ran against a mocked repository that never threw it.`,`The fix wasn't another mock. We flipped the premise: boot the real Program.cs in-memory with WebApplicationFactory, rip out the EF Core registration inside ConfigureTestServices, and rewire it to a genuine Postgres spun up by Testcontainers. Then call POST /users the way a client would — over HTTP — and assert on what comes back: a 200 OK, or a ProblemDetails carrying the real status code and error description.`,`The first honest run caught two bugs the green suite never could: a migration that had never been applied, and a model-binding edge case that only exists on the real pipeline. The suite got slower by about forty seconds of container startup. It also got, for the first time, trustworthy.`],problem:`How do you verify the behavior users actually experience — the real HTTP contract against a real database — without deploying the whole stack every time CI runs?`},tech:{stack:[`ASP.NET Core`,`WebApplicationFactory`,`Mvc.Testing`,`Testcontainers`,`PostgreSQL`,`Docker`,`EF Core`,`FluentAssertions`,`xUnit IAsyncLifetime`],note:`The in-memory host boots the real API pipeline in-process, while Testcontainers wires EF Core to a genuine Postgres container — so every functional test crosses the full HTTP boundary against the real database engine.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: what is a functional test, really?`,body:[`Let's get the vocabulary straight first, because this is where most teams blur the line. Integration tests ask: do these components work when wired together? Functional tests ask: does the system do what the user paid for? For an API, that means the test's only interface is HTTP. We call POST /users, and we judge the response — a 200 OK when the user is created, or a ProblemDetails payload carrying the status code and a description of what went wrong when it isn't.`,`Why not just unit test the handler and call it a day? Because the handler is not the contract. Validation, model binding, routing, exception middleware, serialization — all of that sits between the user and your method, and any one of those layers can silently change what the user experiences. A functional test walks the whole path, so a regression anywhere on it turns red.`],quote:`Users don't call your handlers — they call HTTP. So test the HTTP.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Unit tests`,`Milliseconds fast, pinpoint failures`,`Never see HTTP or DB reality`,`Domain logic, validators`],[`Integration (mocked deps)`,`Fast, verifies wiring`,`Mocks can drift from real behavior`,`Service composition checks`],[`Functional (in-memory host)`,`Real pipeline, real contract`,`Slower, needs Docker infra`,`User-visible API behavior`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: booting the real app in-memory`,body:[`Here's the trick that makes this cheap: we don't deploy anything. The Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing package boots your actual Program inside the test process — real routing, real middleware, real DI — but in-memory. To get at it, you write a FunctionalTestWebAppFactory that extends WebApplicationFactory with your Program type. One prerequisite trips everyone on newer .NET versions: Program has to be a public partial class. One line at the bottom of Program.cs, and the factory can see it.`,`Then the fun part: override ConfigureWebHost, and inside it call ConfigureTestServices. This is your steering wheel into the real service collection. Want to swap the database? Remove the existing registrations — all services whose type is DbContextOptions for your ApplicationDbContext — and add them back pointing wherever you want. You're not mocking the app; you're re-pointing the real app at test infrastructure.`],quote:`The factory isn't a mock framework — it's a steering wheel bolted onto the real app.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,at:`1:54`}},{heading:`03 · The database question: why not just fake it?`,body:[`Now the database, and this is where I hear 'why not the EF InMemory or SQLite provider?' constantly. Because they lie in the ways that hurt: no real unique constraints, different transaction semantics, different SQL dialects. Our 2 AM bug was a Postgres unique-constraint violation — the InMemory provider would have happily accepted the duplicate and the test would have stayed green. The only honest database is the database.`,`So Testcontainers: the PostgreSql module gives you a builder where you pin the image, set the default database, username, and password. It boots a real Docker container and hands you a real connection string. Wire it to xUnit's IAsyncLifetime — StartAsync in InitializeAsync, StopAsync in DisposeAsync — and every test class runs against a pristine, real Postgres. Start it once per collection, not per test, or you'll pay the container boot cost over and over.`],quote:`A mocked database tests your assumptions about the database. Testcontainers tests the database.`,table:{headers:[`DB Strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`EF InMemory provider`,`Zero setup, instant`,`No constraints, no real SQL`,`Smoke checks only`],[`SQLite provider swap`,`File-based, fairly fast`,`Different dialect and semantics`,`Legacy convenience`],[`Testcontainers Postgres`,`Real engine, real constraints`,`Seconds of startup, needs Docker`,`Functional tests, CI`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,at:`3:39`}},{heading:`04 · Asserting on the contract, not the internals`,body:[`Once the response comes back, resist the urge to peek inside the app. The assertion lives on the wire: FluentAssertions on the status code, and on the ProblemDetails body — the status code it declares and the description of the error. That's the contract your clients coded against. If you assert on internal state instead, every refactor turns tests red even when behavior is identical, and the suite becomes a tax instead of a safety net.`,`There's a nice side effect: asserting on ProblemDetails forces you to design your error contract deliberately. What status code does a duplicate email get? What does the description say? Those are product decisions, and the functional test is exactly where they get pinned down.`],quote:`Assert on the receipt the client receives, not on what happened in the kitchen.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,at:`4:18`}},{heading:`05 · What this buys you — and what it costs`,body:[`Let's be honest about the cost, because this isn't free. Container startup is seconds, not milliseconds, and the in-memory host still boots the full pipeline. The discipline that keeps this sane: functional tests cover user-visible endpoints and their contracts; everything combinatorial — validation edge cases, domain rules — stays down in unit tests. If your functional suite creeps past roughly ten minutes in CI, people start skipping it, and a skipped suite is worse than none.`,`What you buy with those seconds is the ability to refactor fearlessly. Swap a handler, change an ORM mapping, restructure middleware — if the HTTP contract holds, the suite stays green; if it breaks, you find out in CI instead of at 09:15 during the morning rush.`],quote:`Fast suites get run. Honest suites get trusted. Functional tests buy trust — budget for it.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Cost`,`Mitigation`],rows:[[`Container startup`,`5–15s per class`,`One container per collection via IAsyncLifetime`],[`Suite runtime`,`Minutes, not milliseconds`,`Reserve for user-visible endpoint contracts`],[`CI requirements`,`Docker on build agents`,`Cache images, parallelize test classes`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,at:`5:51`}}],diagrams:[{id:`functional-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Functional Test Topology`,caption:`The test drives the real pipeline in-memory while EF Core is rewired to a disposable Postgres container.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    T["Functional Test<br/>xUnit + FluentAssertions"] --> F["FunctionalTestWebAppFactory<br/>WebApplicationFactory of Program"]
    F -- "IAsyncLifetime" --> C[("Postgres Container<br/>Testcontainers + Docker")]
    F -- "CreateClient()" --> H["In-Memory Host<br/>public partial class Program"]
    subgraph P["Real ASP.NET Core Pipeline"]
        H --> MW["Middleware + Routing + Validation"] --> E["POST /users Endpoint"] --> D["EF Core DbContext"]
    end
    D -- "Npgsql connection string" --> C
    E -- "200 OK / ProblemDetails" --> T`},{id:`test-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One Functional Test, End to End`,caption:`The full lifecycle: container up, services rewired, HTTP request across the real pipeline, contract asserted, container reaped.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant X as xUnit Test
    participant F as WebAppFactory
    participant C as Postgres Container
    participant H as In-Memory Host
    participant E as Users Endpoint
    X->>F: InitializeAsync()
    F->>C: StartAsync — pull image, boot Postgres
    C-->>F: Host port + connection string
    F->>F: ConfigureTestServices — swap DbContextOptions
    X->>H: POST /users (JSON payload)
    H->>E: Bind, validate, execute
    E->>C: INSERT user via EF Core
    C-->>E: Row persisted
    E-->>X: 200 OK
    X->>X: FluentAssertions on status + ProblemDetails
    X->>F: DisposeAsync()
    F->>C: StopAsync — container reaped`}],takeaways:["Add `public partial class Program { }` at the bottom of Program.cs — one line that unlocks WebApplicationFactory on any .NET 6+ API.",`Remove every DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> registration in ConfigureTestServices before re-adding — leftover registrations silently point tests at the wrong database.`,`Start the Postgres container once per test collection via IAsyncLifetime, not per test — saves 5–15 seconds of Docker startup per class.`,`Assert only on the HTTP contract — status code plus the ProblemDetails payload — so refactors that preserve behavior keep the suite green.`,`Budget the functional suite to finish in under ~10 minutes in CI: one test per user-visible endpoint contract, and push combinatorial edge cases back down to unit tests.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — The Complete Guide to Functional Testing in ASP.NET Core (End-to-End Testing)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ASa8wXMXwrQ`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-mh_Eqia3g50`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:13,title:`Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 14:27`,minutes:14,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Azure`,`.NET`,`RAG`],story:{headline:`The Green Suite That Lied: My First Week with Aspire Testing`,paragraphs:[`It was 9:47 PM the night before a customer demo when staging died. The Notes API booted fine, then fell over on the very first request — the connection string environment variable had never been injected, and every unit test was green because the fake repository we'd mocked never once looked at configuration. Two hundred passing tests, and an app that couldn't talk to its own database.`,`So I finally did the thing I'd been putting off: I wrote a real integration test with .NET Aspire. The test project takes a project reference to the AppHost, calls DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder.CreateAsync, and boots the actual topology — real Postgres container, real EF Core migrations, real environment variables. The first run timed out; starting an AppHost is genuinely expensive. The second run, with persistent container lifetimes keeping resources warm behind the scenes, finished in about 20 seconds. Green — and this time the green meant something.`,`It wasn't all wins, though. The scaffolded test project ignored my central package management setup, so I spent the next hour reconciling package versions by hand. And 20 seconds per run means these tests can't be your default inner loop — they're a spotlight you aim at the integration seam, powered by xUnit's IAsyncLifetime so you pay for startup once, not per test.`],problem:`Unit tests with fakes were green while the real integration — environment variables, connection strings, actual SQL — went completely untested, and the new test project's scaffold fought my central package management setup before anything would compile.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire`,`DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder`,`xUnit`,`IAsyncLifetime`,`EF Core`,`Postgres`],note:`The test project boots the real Aspire AppHost — actual Postgres container, EF Core migrations, and injected environment variables — while xUnit's IAsyncLifetime amortizes the expensive startup across the whole test run.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why fake everything and call it a test?`,body:[`Here's the honest question I had to ask myself that night: what were those 200 green unit tests actually proving? The fake repository never read a connection string, never ran a migration, never cared whether an environment variable existed. They tested my logic in a world that doesn't exist. Aspire testing flips the premise — your test project references the AppHost, and DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder.CreateAsync hands you the real distributed application, with Projects.NoteAppHost as your handle to it.`,`And the ergonomics are genuinely pleasant. You write plain Arrange-Act-Assert, kick things off with CancellationToken.None, hit the real API over HTTP, and assert on the real response. Credit to the Aspire team — the docs here are excellent, I barely had to guess. The trade-off is obvious though: fidelity costs seconds, and mocks cost nothing but tell you less. You're buying truth with time.`],quote:`A mock can't lie to you about a connection string it never had.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Mocks & fakes`,`Milliseconds fast, no Docker`,`Blind to env vars, SQL, wiring`,`Logic-heavy units`],[`Aspire integration`,`Real DB, real config, real logs`,`~20s startup, needs containers`,`API-to-DB seam, smoke tests`],[`Both, layered`,`Fast loop plus high fidelity`,`Two setups to maintain`,`Most real services`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mh_Eqia3g50`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what actually boots when you hit Run`,body:[`Let's walk what happens under the hood, because it explains the pain. Your xUnit test calls into the testing builder, which starts your AppHost — the same orchestrator you run in dev. The AppHost then pulls up the Notes API service and a real Postgres container, injects the connection string as an environment variable, and runs your EF Core migrations. That is a lot of machinery standing behind one assert.`,`That machinery is exactly why my first run timed out. Configuring and starting the AppHost is an expensive operation — cold, you're waiting on container pulls, health checks, migrations. The trick I landed on: persistent container lifetimes. With resources already up behind the scenes, my second run completed in about 20 seconds. Still not cheap — but predictable, and predictable is what you actually need in CI.`],quote:`Your test suite is only as fast as the slowest thing it insists on starting.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mh_Eqia3g50`,at:`3:11`}},{heading:`03 · Pay once, test many: IAsyncLifetime to the rescue`,body:[`Twenty seconds per test does not scale — ten tests and you've burned three-plus minutes just booting. xUnit hands you the escape hatch: implement IAsyncLifetime, and its InitializeAsync and DisposeAsync methods let you create the expensive AppHost once, before any test runs, and tear it down after. The startup cost gets amortized across the whole class instead of paid per test.`,`Now, the trade-off you're signing up for: shared state. Tests in the same class run against the same database, so they need unique data per test or careful cleanup. I've settled on a simple rule — share the expensive infrastructure, isolate the data. Every test writes its own rows with unique keys; nobody steps on anybody. That keeps practical isolation without paying the startup tax over and over.`],quote:`Amortize the expensive thing; isolate the cheap things.`,table:{headers:[`Lifetime strategy`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-test AppHost`,`Total isolation`,`20s x N tests, brutal in CI`,`Rarely worth it`],[`IAsyncLifetime per class`,`One startup, shared infra`,`Shared DB state to manage`,`Default choice`],[`Collection fixture`,`One startup across classes`,`Ordering coupling, complexity`,`Large suites, later`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mh_Eqia3g50`,at:`7:00`}},{heading:`04 · Reading the receipts: logs as evidence`,body:[`Here's the part that genuinely surprised me. Open the test log as a file after a run and you get the actual application logs from inside the test — including the SQL that EF Core emitted from the Notes API. I could literally see the INSERT INTO notes statement, which meant I could confirm the full integration path from API to database with my own eyes, not just trust a status code.`,`That's a debugging superpower when a test fails at 3 AM. Was it the migration? The connection string? The query itself? The log tells you. And the same trick extends to configuration: you can assert that your environment variables are actually set — the exact blind spot that killed my staging deploy. It's a small helper library that makes troubleshooting dramatically easier, and I recommend it without hesitation.`],quote:`Green is an opinion; the SQL log is a receipt.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mh_Eqia3g50`,at:`11:56`}},{heading:`05 · When I would NOT reach for it (the honest cons)`,body:[`Let me be blunt about the downsides, because 'surprised' in the title cuts both ways. These tests need Docker wherever they run, they cost roughly 20 seconds of startup even amortized, and the scaffolded test project didn't respect my central package management setup — I had to reconcile versions by hand before anything compiled. If your CI runners are slow or containerless, this approach fights you the whole way.`,`So my rule: Aspire tests are a spotlight, not a floodlight. Keep a handful — the API-to-database seam, one end-to-end smoke path, the env-var assertions that once burned me — and keep everything else in fast unit tests. If your app is a single service with no real infrastructure wiring, or your team won't tolerate a 20-second feedback loop, skip it. For genuinely distributed apps, though? I'm a convert.`],quote:`Aspire tests are a spotlight, not a floodlight — aim them at the seam mocks can't see.`,table:{headers:[`Situation`,`Use Aspire testing?`,`Why`],rows:[[`Distributed app, real DB seam`,`Yes`,`Only way to test the real wiring`],[`Pure domain logic`,`No`,`Unit tests are 100x faster`],[`Containerless CI runners`,`No`,`AppHost needs Docker`],[`Pre-release smoke suite`,`Yes`,`20s is cheap before a demo`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`aspire-test-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`What Aspire Testing Actually Boots`,caption:`The xUnit test project starts the real AppHost, which stands up the Notes API, a live Postgres container, and injected environment variables — and streams logs back to a file you can read as evidence.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Test["xUnit Test Project"]
        T["Integration Test<br/>Arrange · Act · Assert"]
        L["IAsyncLifetime<br/>InitializeAsync / DisposeAsync"]
    end
    B["DistributedApplicationTestingBuilder<br/>.CreateAsync()"]
    subgraph Host["Aspire AppHost"]
        API["Notes API<br/>ASP.NET Core + EF Core"]
        DB[("Postgres Container")]
        EV["Environment Variables<br/>Connection String"]
    end
    LOG["Test Log File<br/>EF Core SQL output"]
    T -->|"project ref to Projects.NoteAppHost"| B
    L -->|"boots once"| B
    B -->|"starts"| Host
    EV -->|"injected into"| API
    API -->|"migrate + INSERT"| DB
    T -->|"HTTP request"| API
    API -->|"app logs"| LOG`},{id:`aspire-test-lifecycle`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Test Lifecycle: Pay for Startup Once`,caption:`IAsyncLifetime front-loads the expensive AppHost start before any test runs, so each test only pays for an HTTP round-trip and a real INSERT — not another 20-second boot.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant X as xUnit Runner
    participant L as IAsyncLifetime
    participant B as TestingBuilder
    participant H as AppHost
    participant P as Postgres
    X->>L: InitializeAsync()
    L->>B: CreateAsync()
    B->>H: start AppHost
    H->>P: start container and run migrations
    H-->>L: resources ready, env vars set
    X->>H: HTTP request to Notes API
    H->>P: INSERT INTO notes
    P-->>H: row persisted
    H-->>X: 200 OK
    X->>L: DisposeAsync()
    L->>H: shut down or keep warm`}],takeaways:[`Budget 15-25 seconds for a cold AppHost start and roughly 20s warm with persistent container lifetimes — set CI timeouts above 60s so a slow pull never fails the build.`,`Implement IAsyncLifetime so the AppHost starts once per test class: 20s amortized over 10 tests is 2s each, versus 200s+ paid per-test.`,`Keep Aspire integration tests to roughly 5-10% of your suite — a handful aimed at the API-to-DB seam, never a replacement for unit tests.`,`Never trust a green check alone: open the test log and confirm the actual EF Core SQL (e.g. INSERT INTO) plus that environment variables are set.`,`Wire the test project into central package management on day one — reconciling scaffolded package versions after the fact costs an hour you don't have.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Here's What I Think About Aspire Testing (honestly... surprised)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mh_Eqia3g50`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-ejA6HiIobb0`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:14,title:`How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 11:04`,minutes:11,difficulty:3,tags:[`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`One-Line Upgrade: How .NET 9 → 10 Went From Week-Long Plague to Coffee Break`,paragraphs:[`Last November, the .NET 9 upgrade ate my entire week. Five projects in the solution, each with its own hand-pinned package versions, and the moment I touched a csproj, git lit up with merge conflicts because three teammates were referencing the same NuGet package at three different versions. Staging restored fine; the production build pulled a version that didn't exist anywhere in the other projects' graphs. Two days of version archaeology before the thing even compiled.`,`This year, .NET 10 — the new long-term support release, out for about a month — landed, and I gave myself a challenge: upgrade before my coffee went cold. One line in Directory.Build.props flipped every project to net10.0. One pass over Directory.Packages.props bumped the package versions. Dockerfile base images went 9.0 → 10.0. Build, tests, green. Minutes, not days.`,`The bonus round: I finally evicted the old .sln file — that GUID-studded relic nobody can actually read — for the new XML-based .slnx format. Two clicks in Visual Studio 2026, and my solution file became something I can review line-by-line in a pull request.`],problem:`Scattered per-project config — TargetFramework and package versions duplicated across every csproj — turns a routine LTS upgrade into days of merge conflicts and version-drift bugs where the same package lands at different versions in different projects.`},tech:{stack:[`.NET 10`,`Visual Studio 2026`,`Directory.Build.props`,`Directory.Packages.props`,`.slnx`,`Docker`],note:`The centralized-config trio — Build.props for build settings, Packages.props for versions, .slnx for the solution manifest — plus VS 2026 and Docker, the pieces that shrink a framework upgrade from days to minutes.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why does a one-line change still scare people?`,body:[`Here's the thing — .NET 10 is the LTS, it's been out about a month, and honestly most of you watching are still 'late to the party.' That's fine. But let's be honest about why upgrades get postponed: it's never the framework swap itself. Changing TargetFramework is trivial. The fear is the blast radius — opening every csproj, hand-editing five files, praying the package versions line up.`,`So the real engineering question isn't 'how do I upgrade?' It's 'how do I make the upgrade surface area equal to one file?' If your TargetFramework lives in one place and your package versions live in one place, the scary annual ritual collapses into a diff you can read on your phone.`],quote:`The framework swap is one line; everything else is self-inflicted.`,table:{headers:[`Upgrade surface`,`Per-project config`,`Central config`],rows:[[`TargetFramework`,`Edit every csproj`,`One line in Directory.Build.props`],[`Package versions`,`N files, merge conflicts`,`One Directory.Packages.props`],[`New project onboarding`,`Copy-paste boilerplate`,`Inherits automatically`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejA6HiIobb0`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: Directory.Build.props is the single source of truth`,body:[`Directory.Build.props has been around since 2017 — nearly a decade — and I still meet senior devs who've never used it. The idea is simple: drop one file at the solution root, and every project underneath inherits it. TargetFramework, ImplicitUsings, Nullable, static code analysis settings — all centralized, all deleted from the individual project files.`,`Why not just keep editing each csproj? Because every new project you add becomes a fresh chance to drift. With the props file, a new project falls back to the centralized configuration automatically — you literally do nothing. And if one weird project genuinely needs an override, the folder hierarchy lets you scope it without polluting the rest.`,`So what does upgrading to .NET 10 actually look like here? I open Directory.Build.props, change the target framework, and we're basically done. That's the whole move.`],quote:`If the same property appears in two csproj files, one of them is already wrong.`,table:{headers:[`Setting`,`Lives in`,`Why centralize it`],rows:[[`TargetFramework`,`Directory.Build.props`,`One-line version bumps every November`],[`ImplicitUsings / Nullable`,`Directory.Build.props`,`Consistent language defaults everywhere`],[`Static code analysis`,`Directory.Build.props`,`Same quality bar for all 5 projects`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejA6HiIobb0`,at:`1:32`}},{heading:`03 · Central package management: one file owns the versions`,body:[`Next layer: Directory.Packages.props, available since .NET 6. This is central package management — you declare every NuGet version once, in one file, and your projects reference packages by name only. The csproj says 'I need Serilog'; the props file decides how old it is.`,`This kills the two bugs that ruined my .NET 9 upgrade. First, merge conflicts: when the same package is referenced in multiple projects, everyone edits the same central line instead of colliding across five files. Second, version drift: it becomes structurally impossible for two projects to silently use different versions of the same package.`,`The upgrade workflow is right there in the IDE: right-click the solution, Manage NuGet Packages for Solution, bump the versions — and everything is nicely organized in one props file. One review, one diff, one source of truth.`],quote:`Your csproj declares what it needs; one file decides how old it is.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Per-project versions`,`Familiar, local edits`,`Merge conflicts, version drift`,`Tiny 1–2 project repos`],[`Central package management`,`One version truth, clean diffs`,`One-time migration effort`,`Any multi-project solution`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejA6HiIobb0`,at:`3:41`}},{heading:`04 · The .slnx migration: draining the GUID soup`,body:[`Now the bonus topic — the solution file itself. The old .sln describes solution folders, projects, their relative paths, and then a pile of GUIDs representing project references. It's a bespoke format that's genuinely complicated, and worse, it's hostile to diffs: two people adding projects collide on GUID blocks in every merge.`,`Microsoft's answer is .slnx — an XML-based solution format that looks a lot like your project files already do. Same information, human-readable, line-diffable. When a teammate adds a project, your pull request shows exactly that, not a GUID avalanche.`,`Migration is deliberately boring: two simple options in Visual Studio 2026, the easiest being the File menu on your selected solution. Is there a trade-off? Sure — muscle memory, and some older tooling still expects .sln. But readability and merge-friendliness win, and the conversion is reversible enough that there's no reason to keep suffering.`],quote:`A solution file you can read in a code review is a solution file worth having.`,table:{headers:[``,`.sln`,`.slnx`],rows:[[`Format`,`Custom, GUID-heavy`,`XML, csproj-like`],[`Merge conflicts`,`GUID blocks collide`,`Line-based, readable diffs`],[`Migration`,`—`,`Built into Visual Studio 2026`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejA6HiIobb0`,at:`7:21`}},{heading:`05 · Shipping it: Docker, VS 2026, and the ten-minute checklist`,body:[`Two footguns before you call it done. First, tooling: you need Visual Studio 2026 to run .NET 10 — older IDEs will leave you staring at an unsupported framework. Second, the container: if your Dockerfile still says aspnet:9.0, you'll compile on .NET 10 and ship on .NET 9. Bump every FROM tag in the same pull request.`,`Then it's just build and test. With the centralized config in place, the entire mechanical upgrade — TFM, packages, Docker tags, verification — fits in about ten minutes. The habit matters more than this release: every future November gets cheap, because the upgrade surface stays at one file per concern.`,`And that's the meta-lesson. The teams that upgrade on release day aren't braver than you — their config is just centralized, so there's nothing to be afraid of.`],quote:`An upgrade that fits in a coffee break is an upgrade you'll actually do on release day.`,table:{headers:[`Step`,`Where`,`Cost`],rows:[[`Flip TFM to net10.0`,`Directory.Build.props`,`~1 min`],[`Bump package versions`,`Directory.Packages.props`,`~5 min`],[`Bump Docker base images`,`Dockerfile(s)`,`~2 min`],[`Build + tests`,`Visual Studio 2026 / CLI`,`~5 min`]]}}],diagrams:[{id:`central-config-topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Centralized build & package config topology`,caption:`One props file per concern feeds every project under the solution root — new projects inherit for free, and the .slnx manifest lists them all in readable XML.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    subgraph ROOT["Solution root"]
        B["Directory.Build.props<br/>TargetFramework · ImplicitUsings · Nullable · analyzers"]
        P["Directory.Packages.props<br/>every NuGet version, declared once"]
        S["app.slnx<br/>XML solution manifest"]
    end
    subgraph SRC["src — 5 projects"]
        W["Web API"]
        L["Class libraries"]
        K["Worker services"]
    end
    B -->|inherited| W
    B -->|inherited| L
    B -->|inherited| K
    P -->|version pins| W
    P -->|version pins| L
    P -->|version pins| K
    S -.->|lists projects| W
    S -.->|lists projects| L
    S -.->|lists projects| K`},{id:`upgrade-path`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`The ten-minute .NET 9 → 10 upgrade path`,caption:`Every step touches exactly one centralized file — no csproj spelunking, no version drift, and the .slnx conversion rides along as an optional final hop.`,mermaid:`flowchart TD
    A["Start: .NET 9 solution"] --> B["Directory.Build.props<br/>net9.0 to net10.0"]
    B --> C["Manage NuGet Packages for Solution<br/>bump versions in Directory.Packages.props"]
    C --> D["Dockerfile base images<br/>sdk/aspnet 9.0 to 10.0"]
    D --> E["dotnet build + dotnet test<br/>Visual Studio 2026 / .NET 10 SDK"]
    E -->|green| F["Commit — upgrade done in minutes"]
    E -->|red| G["Fix rare breaking changes"]
    G --> E
    F -.->|optional| H["Convert .sln to .slnx<br/>File menu, Visual Studio 2026"]`}],takeaways:[`Put TargetFramework in Directory.Build.props and a framework upgrade becomes a one-line diff — one edit instead of one per csproj.`,`Adopt Directory.Packages.props (central package management, .NET 6+) so every package has exactly one version — it structurally eliminates cross-project drift and the merge conflicts that come with it.`,`Budget ~10 minutes for the mechanical upgrade (TFM + packages + Docker tags) and spend the saved days on breaking-change triage instead of config archaeology.`,`Migrate .sln to .slnx: XML diffs line-by-line in pull requests instead of colliding on GUID blocks — two clicks from the File menu in Visual Studio 2026.`,`Bump every Dockerfile FROM tag (sdk/aspnet 9.0 → 10.0) in the same PR as the TFM change, or you'll compile on .NET 10 and ship on .NET 9.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — How I Upgraded to .NET 10 in Minutes + New .slnx Format`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ejA6HiIobb0`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`},{id:`mj--73fAqw8ckU`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:15,title:`Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 17:02`,minutes:17,difficulty:3,tags:[`Postgres`,`Docker`,`.NET`],story:{headline:`The Night Our Compose File Became Config Archaeology`,paragraphs:[`It's 11 PM the night before a stakeholder demo. The reporting API won't start — MassTransit can't reach RabbitMQ. I grep the compose file: the broker is there, ports are mapped, the healthcheck is green. Forty minutes later I find it: someone 'cleaned up' the credentials, the API's environment block still says guest/guest, and the container now boots with a generated password. Three sources of truth, all disagreeing, all technically correct.`,`That's the moment you realize Docker Compose was never the problem — scale was. At two containers it's a blessing. At five — two APIs, a Blazor WebAssembly front end, Postgres, RabbitMQ, pgAdmin — it's archaeology. Every connection string lives in three places: the YAML, appsettings.json, and someone's Slack message from last March.`,`So we migrated the whole thing to a .NET Aspire AppHost. I expected an orchestration swap. What I didn't expect was resilience, OpenTelemetry, service discovery, and a live dashboard — basically for free — as the bonus for finally describing our application in C# instead of YAML.`],problem:`Two ASP.NET APIs chatting over MassTransit on RabbitMQ, a Blazor WASM client, Postgres with content and reporting schemas — all held together by a Docker Compose file that had quietly become the single point of failure in our dev loop.`},tech:{stack:[`Docker Compose`,`.NET Aspire`,`Postgres`,`RabbitMQ`,`MassTransit`,`Blazor WASM`,`OpenTelemetry`,`pgAdmin`],note:`The dev-loop moving parts: two ASP.NET Core APIs exchanging messages via MassTransit on RabbitMQ, Postgres holding the content and reporting schemas, and a Blazor WebAssembly client — orchestrated by Docker Compose yesterday, by a .NET Aspire AppHost with OpenTelemetry today.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why our compose file became a liability`,body:[`Here's my first thought, live: Docker Compose didn't fail us — it succeeded us right past its sweet spot. Two containers? Beautiful. But the moment you're wiring two APIs, a Blazor WASM client, Postgres, RabbitMQ, and pgAdmin, that YAML is doing a job it was never designed for: being the brain of a distributed application.`,`Why not just tidy the YAML? Because tidiness isn't the disease — duplication is. The same connection string lives in the compose environment block, in appsettings.json, and in the README. Change one, forget two, and you get my 11 PM. And there's no service discovery: every host:port is a hand-typed fact that can rot.`,`So the real question isn't 'which orchestrator is cooler.' It's: where does the truth about my application's topology live? Compose says: in a file that can't see your code. Aspire says: in C#, next to the code, with typed references.`],quote:`A compose file describes containers. Your app needs a description of itself.`,table:{headers:[`Concern`,`Docker Compose`,`.NET Aspire`,`Verdict`],rows:[[`Connection strings`,`Hand-written env vars`,`Injected via WithReference`,`Aspire`],[`Service discovery`,`Hardcoded host:port`,`Resolved by resource name`,`Aspire`],[`Telemetry`,`DIY OTel wiring`,`Service defaults, out of the box`,`Aspire`],[`Production deploys`,`Battle-tested everywhere`,`Dev-loop focused`,`Compose/K8s`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: what Aspire actually replaces`,body:[`Walking the boundary now: what does Aspire actually replace? Not Docker — the containers still run. It replaces the orchestration layer and, more importantly, the YAML as source of truth. The AppHost becomes a composition root in C#: AddPostgres with a pgAdmin companion, AddRabbitMQ with WithManagementPlugin for the management UI, AddProject for each service, WithReference to wire them together.`,`Why not run both — compose for prod, Aspire for dev? You will, and that's fine, as long as you accept that only one of them is the dev-loop truth. We deleted compose from the inner loop entirely, because keeping two sources of truth alive is exactly how the 11 PM story gets a sequel.`,`And the honest boundary: Aspire is not a production deploy tool. It models and runs your distributed app locally, hands you the dashboard, and gets you cloud-ready defaults. Kubernetes still owns prod. Migrating to Aspire is an inner-loop upgrade, not a platform swap.`],quote:`Aspire didn't replace my containers — it replaced the YAML I used to lie with.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,at:`4:54`}},{heading:`03 · The free wins hiding in AddServiceDefaults`,body:[`Now the part I genuinely didn't expect: AddServiceDefaults. One call per project — and suddenly every service has /health and /alive endpoints, OpenTelemetry exporting traces, logs, and metrics, service discovery wired into HttpClient, and resilience handlers on outgoing calls. That's the 'bonus' for migrating, and it's very real.`,`The Aspire dashboard is where this pays off. Resources with live status, structured logs you can filter per service, traces that show the MassTransit hop from the content API to the reporting API end to end. No more docker logs -f across five terminals while praying.`,`Why not just install the OpenTelemetry packages myself? You can — I had partially done exactly that. But per-project DIY setup drifts, and drift is the enemy. Centralizing it in service defaults means a new service is observable before it writes its first line of business logic.`],quote:`Resilience you didn't wire is resilience you don't have.`,table:{headers:[`Capability`,`What you get`,`DIY effort`],rows:[[`Health checks`,`/health and /alive mapped`,`Medium`],[`OpenTelemetry`,`Traces, logs, metrics to dashboard`,`High`],[`Service discovery`,`HttpClient resolves by name`,`Medium`],[`Resilience`,`Retry and timeout on HTTP calls`,`High`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,at:`8:59`}},{heading:`04 · The Blazor WASM gotcha that cost me an afternoon`,body:[`Every migration has its gotcha, and ours wore a Blazor badge. I try to add Aspire orchestration support to the WebAssembly project and Visual Studio smiles back: 'the .NET Aspire orchestration project is already up to date.' Translation: nothing happened, and it won't tell you why.`,`The fix is almost embarrassingly low-tech: add the project reference by hand — from the AppHost to the Blazor WASM project. In Visual Studio you can literally drag one project onto the other. The source generators kick in behind the scenes, and suddenly AddProject for the front end just works, endpoints and all.`,`I love this one because it's a general lesson: when a wizard refuses, drop down to the fundamentals before you fight the dialog. Project references are the mechanism; the wizard is just a convenience that happens to have a blind spot.`],quote:`When the wizard says 'already up to date,' it means 'you're on your own.'`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Re-run orchestration wizard`,`One click`,`Silently no-ops for WASM`,`Avoid`],[`Manual project reference`,`Works every time`,`One manual step`,`Do this`],[`Leave WASM out of AppHost`,`Zero effort`,`Loses one-key F5 of full system`,`Avoid`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,at:`13:10`}},{heading:`05 · Living in the dashboard: pgAdmin, RabbitMQ, and the truth about env vars`,body:[`Last block, and it's the one that changes your daily muscle memory: living inside the dashboard. Click the pgAdmin endpoint and you're in the database UI, content and reporting schemas staring back at you. Click the RabbitMQ endpoint and the management UI opens — MassTransit's auto-declared exchanges all visible.`,`Then the trick that sold my whole team: I log out of RabbitMQ and realize nobody remembers the password. In the old world, that's a Slack archaeology dig. In Aspire, I click the resource in the dashboard, scroll to the environment variables, and read the username and password the AppHost injected at startup. Thirty seconds, zero secrets files.`,`That's the real UX shift. The dashboard isn't a log viewer bolted on the side — it's the control tower: resources, credentials, health, logs, traces, and one-click launches into every admin UI in the system.`],quote:`The dashboard isn't a log viewer — it's the control tower for the whole distributed app.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,at:`14:10`}}],diagrams:[{id:`topology`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`Content Platform topology under the Aspire AppHost`,caption:`Same containers as the compose days — but now every wire is a typed WithReference, and all telemetry lands in one dashboard.`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph Browser["Browser"]
        UI["Blazor WebAssembly"]
    end
    subgraph Aspire["Aspire AppHost orchestrates"]
        API["Content Platform API"]
        RPT["Reporting API"]
        MQ["RabbitMQ + mgmt UI"]
        PG["Postgres"]
        PGA["pgAdmin"]
        DASH["Aspire Dashboard"]
    end
    UI -->|"HTTPS"| API
    API -->|"MassTransit"| MQ
    RPT -->|"MassTransit"| MQ
    API -->|"Npgsql"| PG
    RPT -->|"Npgsql"| PG
    API -. "OTLP" .-> DASH
    RPT -. "OTLP" .-> DASH
    PGA --- PG`},{id:`startup`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`F5 to fully-wired system: the AppHost startup sequence`,caption:`One keypress starts Postgres, RabbitMQ, both APIs, and the dashboard — connection strings and service discovery injected, not typed.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant Dev as Developer
    participant AH as AppHost
    participant PG as Postgres
    participant MQ as RabbitMQ
    participant API as Content API
    participant D as Dashboard

    Dev->>AH: F5 / dotnet run
    AH->>PG: start container, inject connection string
    AH->>MQ: start container + management plugin
    AH->>API: launch with service discovery env
    API->>PG: connect via resolved reference
    API->>MQ: MassTransit declares exchanges
    API-->>D: OTLP traces, logs, metrics
    AH-->>D: resource health + console logs`}],takeaways:[`Past ~4 hand-wired services, a compose file becomes config archaeology — migrating to an Aspire AppHost deletes the duplicated connection strings and the YAML-as-truth problem in one move.`,`One AddServiceDefaults call per project buys health endpoints (/health, /alive), OTel export, service discovery, and HttpClient resilience — measure it in lines, not days.`,`Blazor WASM won't join orchestration via the wizard: add the AppHost-to-WASM project reference manually, then AddProject just works.`,`Budget the migration in hours, not weeks: AppHost, resources, references, and service defaults are an afternoon — and the dashboard-driven credential/debug workflow saves you minutes every single day after that.`,`Keep Aspire in the inner loop and Compose/Kubernetes in production — Aspire is the dev-loop upgrade, not a deploy replacement.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — Migrating From Docker Compose to .NET Aspire (my experience)`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-73fAqw8ckU`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[{id:`fundamental-deployment`,note:`The written version of the same idea — the long-form lesson this video compresses.`}],status:`ready`},{id:`mj-zvBu0OOCVos`,kind:`video`,pathId:`dotnet-ai-and-craft`,order:16,title:`I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,subtitle:`Milan Jovanovic · 12:06`,minutes:12,difficulty:3,tags:[`.NET`,`Cache`],story:{headline:`Fourteen containers, one failing test, and a CI log that told me nothing`,paragraphs:[`It's 11 PM on a release Friday and the integration suite is red in CI — one test, POST /orders, timing out — and it only fails on the build agent. Locally, everything is green. My Aspire app host boots all fourteen resources just to exercise that one endpoint: Redis, Postgres, three APIs, two workers, a queue. Four minutes of startup per attempt, and the only diagnostic I get is a stack trace and a shrug.`,`Here's the part that stings. Locally I can watch the Aspire dashboard — traces, structured logs, the whole story of the request. In CI, the dashboard dies with the agent. No artifact, no replay, nothing to download. So I do what we all do: re-run the pipeline three times, sprinkle Console.WriteLine like salt, and hope run number four comes back green.`,`That's why reading the 2025-26 Aspire roadmap felt personal. Partial app host execution. Dashboard capture and replay from CI. Request redirection and mocking. Friendly local DNS names like redis.localhost. It reads like a list of the exact walls I hit that night — written by the team that owns the other side of the wall.`],problem:`How do you run and observe only the slice of a distributed system you actually need — locally and in CI — without dragging all fourteen resources along for the ride?`},tech:{stack:[`.NET Aspire`,`AppHost`,`Aspire Dashboard`,`WithExplicitStart`,`Testcontainers`,`WireMock`,`OpenTelemetry`],note:`The moving parts the roadmap touches: the AppHost that orchestrates every resource, the Dashboard that observes them, and the test/CI tooling — with Testcontainers and WireMock as the incumbents Aspire is now challenging.`},reasoning:[{heading:`01 · First thought: why 'start everything' murders your feedback loop`,body:[`Right now, when you start the Aspire app host, every resource comes up. All fourteen containers spinning so I can hit one endpoint. David Fowler is quick to point out that WithExplicitStart already exists — you mark a resource, and it stays idle until you manually start it from the dashboard. That's the escape hatch today, and honestly, most teams have never heard of it.`,`The roadmap formalizes this into partial app host execution, including for tests. The trade-off is full fidelity versus a fast loop. Why not just docker-compose up the subset you need? Because then you lose the things Aspire actually gives you — service discovery, telemetry correlation, lifecycle management. The win isn't skipping orchestration; it's keeping orchestration while shrinking the blast radius.`],quote:`Your feedback loop is the smallest unit of developer productivity — defend it like production uptime.`,table:{headers:[`Approach`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Full app host`,`Complete fidelity, real deps`,`Slow startup, heavy RAM`,`Final pre-merge check`],[`WithExplicitStart`,`Available today, fine control`,`Manual toggling every run`,`Debugging one slice`],[`Partial execution (roadmap)`,`Fast, declarative subsets`,`Not shipped yet`,`Everyday dev + tests`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvBu0OOCVos`,at:`0:00`}},{heading:`02 · Walking the boundary: friendly names vs. port roulette`,body:[`Every app host run hands out fresh ports. localhost:5432 becomes localhost:5711 tomorrow, and any config file, seed script, or teammate's curl command pointed at the old port silently rots. The roadmap's local DNS support fixes this with friendly names — redis.localhost, api.localhost — that survive restarts and parallel runs.`,`Why not just pin the ports? Because pinned ports collide the moment two app hosts run side by side, which is exactly what happens when your test suite runs while you're debugging. Names are the cheapest abstraction in networking, and Aspire is finally handing them out locally. Small feature, permanent win.`],quote:`Stable names are the cheapest configuration management you will ever ship.`,source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvBu0OOCVos`,at:`2:38`}},{heading:`03 · The testing bet: a red pipeline you can replay`,body:[`This is the section I'd bet on. Capture the dashboard state during a CI run, download it as an artifact, replay it locally — that turns a red pipeline from a riddle into a recording. Add the live dashboard view while tests execute, and you can literally watch service behavior as the assertions run.`,`Request redirection and mocking is the WireMock pattern brought inside the app host: stub the payment provider, the email API, whatever third party keeps making your tests flaky. Honest confession: I've stuck with Testcontainers for integration tests because it's been enough. But capture-and-replay is a capability Testcontainers doesn't give me, and that might tip the scales. Code coverage support, on the other hand? Existing tooling already covers it.`],quote:`A failure you can replay is worth ten you can only re-run.`,table:{headers:[`Option`,`Pros`,`Cons`,`Fit`],rows:[[`Testcontainers`,`Mature, simple, proven`,`No dashboard, no replay`,`Current default`],[`Aspire testing today`,`Real topology, telemetry`,`CI runs are black boxes`,`Aspire-native apps`],[`Aspire + capture/replay`,`Replayable CI failures, live view`,`Roadmap, not released`,`The bet to watch`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvBu0OOCVos`,at:`4:37`}},{heading:`04 · Where I'd push back: not every roadmap line deserves your sprint`,body:[`Not every line on a roadmap deserves your sprint. Code coverage support is nice, but coverage tooling already exists and works — adopting Aspire's version buys you almost nothing. Executing commands inside containers is genuinely powerful for debugging, but it's also how snowflake environments are born: if your recovery step lives only in someone's terminal history, you don't have a recovery step.`,`So here's my ordering. Partial execution first — biggest daily ROI, it compounds on every single run. Capture and replay second — biggest CI ROI, it converts 3 AM mysteries into recordings. Local DNS third — tiny fix, permanent win. Everything else, adopt opportunistically as it ships. Adopt by feedback-loop ROI, not by release date.`],quote:`Adopt roadmap items by feedback-loop ROI, not by release date.`,table:{headers:[`Roadmap item`,`Effort to adopt`,`Payoff`,`My call`],rows:[[`Partial app host execution`,`Low`,`High — every run`,`Adopt first`],[`Dashboard capture/replay`,`Low`,`High — CI debugging`,`Adopt second`],[`Local DNS names`,`Trivial`,`Medium — permanent`,`Adopt on arrival`],[`Container commands`,`Medium`,`Situational`,`Sparingly`],[`Code coverage support`,`Low`,`Low — tooling exists`,`Skip for now`]]},source:{label:`Milan Jovanovic — I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvBu0OOCVos`,at:`5:21`}}],diagrams:[{id:`apphost-modes`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`One AppHost, two modes: full start vs. partial execution`,caption:`The same AppHost serves the dev loop (start everything, or hold resources idle with WithExplicitStart) and the CI test run (partial execution, stubbed externals, captured dashboard state).`,mermaid:`flowchart LR
    subgraph LOCAL["Local dev loop"]
        DEV["Developer"] --> AH["Aspire AppHost"]
        AH -->|"default: start all"| API["API service"]
        AH -->|"default: start all"| WKR["Worker service"]
        AH -->|"WithExplicitStart"| OPT["Optional service (idle)"]
        API --> RED[("Redis<br/>redis.localhost")]
        API --> PG[("Postgres<br/>db.localhost")]
    end
    API -. "OTEL traces" .-> DASH["Aspire Dashboard"]
    WKR -. "OTEL traces" .-> DASH
    subgraph CIRUN["CI test run"]
        TH["Aspire test host"] -->|"partial app host"| API
        TH -->|"request redirection"| STUB["External API stub"]
        TH -->|"capture state"| ART["Dashboard artifact"]
    end
    ART -->|"download + replay"| DEV`},{id:`capture-replay`,kind:`mermaid`,title:`CI failure to local replay`,caption:`How dashboard capture turns a red pipeline into a recording you can scrub on your own machine — the exact flow that would have saved my release Friday.`,mermaid:`sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant CI as CI Agent
    participant TH as Aspire Test Host
    participant DH as Aspire Dashboard
    participant AR as Artifact Store
    participant DV as Developer Laptop

    CI->>TH: Run integration tests
    TH->>DH: Start partial app host (needed services only)
    TH->>DH: Stream telemetry during run
    DH-->>TH: Live dashboard view
    Note over TH,DH: Watch service behavior as tests execute
    TH->>AR: Capture dashboard state (on failure)
    AR-->>DV: Download artifact
    DV->>DH: Replay captured run locally
    DV->>DV: Inspect traces, logs, structured data`}],takeaways:[`If your app host boots more than 5 resources for one test, you're paying 30+ seconds of startup per run — use WithExplicitStart today, partial execution when it ships.`,`Switch to friendly DNS names (redis.localhost) the day they land; stable names kill the port-rot bug class that costs roughly 1 hour per developer per week.`,`Capture dashboard state on every red CI run — a replayable artifact cuts diagnosis time from hours to minutes.`,`Stub third-party dependencies via request redirection; flaky externals cause up to 20% of false-red builds.`,`Rank roadmap adoption by feedback-loop ROI: partial execution > capture/replay > local DNS > container commands > code coverage.`],sources:[{kind:`youtube`,label:`Milan Jovanovic — I reviewed the .NET Aspire 2025‑26 Roadmap. Here are my thoughts`,url:`https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zvBu0OOCVos`,author:`Milan Jovanovic`,note:`The talk itself. Every section below links to the minute it starts.`}],alsoCoveredBy:[],status:`ready`}];export{e as lessons};